# 5. Functional Requirements

This section defines every functional requirement for Phase 1, organised by module. Each requirement has a unique ID for traceability, a user story, testable acceptance criteria, business rules, and edge case handling. The platform modules (P01–P06) are documented first because they are dependencies for the functional modules.

Cross-reference: every entity referenced in any requirement below is defined in Section 4 (Data Model). Every entity referenced in any user story is also subject to the RBAC matrix in Section 3.

## 5.1 Platform Modules (P01–P06)

### 5.1.1 P01 — Workflow Engine

**FR-P01-001: Configurable workflow definitions**

**Description: **A single workflow engine drives all approval and notification processes across all modules. Workflow definitions are configuration data stored in the workflows entity. Adding a new workflow type requires no code deployment. The full set of flows configured for Phase 1 is registered in Appendix I — Configured Flow Catalog.

**User story:** ****As an Organisation Admin, I want to configure new approval workflows through the platform***’****s configuration interface so that I can introduce new business processes without engineering effort.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Workflow definitions persist in the workflows entity with at minimum: workflow_code, pattern, stages_definition, sla_default_days.

- On save, a workflow validation step checks: at least one stage defined, every stage has an assignee resolution rule, SLA values are positive integers.

- A new workflow becomes available to its owning module immediately on save with status = ACTIVE.

- In-flight workflow_instances continue to run on the version they were created with; only new instances pick up the latest version.

**Business rules:**

- Five workflow patterns supported: SEQUENTIAL, PARALLEL_ALL_OF, PARALLEL_ANY_OF, CONDITIONAL, DYNAMIC_APPROVER. The engine supports all five patterns. The Phase 1 configured set (Appendix I) exercises three as top-level workflow modes — SEQUENTIAL (Series), PARALLEL_ALL_OF (Parallel), and CONDITIONAL — while PARALLEL_ANY_OF and DYNAMIC_APPROVER are realised through approval-flow decision-matrix and exception-level routing rather than as a distinct workflow mode.

- Approver resolution supports four mechanisms: named role, reporting chain position, named individual, cost centre head.

- Where a workflow references ‘Manager’ as approver, the system resolves to L1 Manager by default. For employees whose L1 Manager is above a configurable seniority threshold, resolution escalates to HR Admin.

- HOD, Skip-level Manager, and Dotted Line Manager roles do not receive workflow approval authority by default. HOD approval authority is configurable per workflow type per HOD user by Organisation Admin. UAG Head workflow approval authority is a hard ceiling — cannot be granted under any configuration.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Save of a workflow definition with a circular reference between stages is rejected with a validation error.

- Attempting to delete a workflow with active workflow_instances is rejected; the workflow can only be deprecated, and its definition retained for the lifetime of any in-flight instance.

- If a named-individual approver becomes inactive, the system surfaces a configuration error on the next instance instantiation and falls back to the role-based resolution if available.

**FR-P01-002: SLA with auto-escalation**

**Description: **Each workflow stage carries a configurable SLA. Breach of SLA triggers an automatic escalation to the next level. Auto-approval and auto-rejection on escalation timeout are configurable per workflow type but disabled platform-wide for leave and attendance per locked decisions.

**User story: ***As a Workflow Engine, I want to enforce SLA timers and escalation rules so that approval requests do not stagnate indefinitely.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- When a stage exceeds its configured SLA without action, the system fires an escalation event.

- Escalation event creates entries in security_audit_log and workflow_actions (action_type = ESCALATE).

- Escalation notifies: the original approver (reminder) plus the configured escalation targets (next-level manager, HRBP, HR Admin, etc. per stage definition).

- Auto-approval and auto-rejection on escalation timeout are configurable per workflow but disabled by default. Leave (M04) and attendance (M05) workflows are platform-locked at disabled.

**Business rules:**

- Leave standard flow escalates after 10 days of inaction. Attendance flow escalates after 5 days. Separation workflow escalates after 5 days.

- On payroll cutoff days (24th and 25th of each month), attendance escalation cadence overrides to 2-hourly notifications.

- Auto-approval is disabled for all attendance request types — this is a deliberate override of the Darwinbox source defaults and must not be restored during migration configuration.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- When clock changes (DST or system clock adjustment) cause an SLA timer to fire prematurely, the system rolls forward to the next valid 00:01 UTC and fires once.

- If the configured next-level escalation target is also inactive, escalation cascades to the next valid target up the chain; if no valid target exists, the request surfaces in the Organisation Admin’s exception queue.

**FR-P01-003: Delegation with conflict-of-interest check**

**Description: **Approvers can delegate authority to another user for a date range. The system enforces a conflict-of-interest check — the delegate cannot be an originator or downstream party in the same request.

**User story: ***As an Approver, I want to delegate my approval authority during leave or absence so that requests routed to me continue to be processed.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Delegation creates a workflow_action with action_type = DELEGATE referencing delegate_to_user_id and a date range.

- All requests routed to the original approver during the delegation window resolve to the delegate.

- The conflict-of-interest check rejects delegation if the delegate is the requestor, the immediate downstream approver, or in a conflict-of-interest relationship.

- The original approver remains visible in the audit trail; the delegate’s action is logged as ‘delegated authority of {original}’.

**Business rules:**

- Delegation cannot extend past the original approver’s contract_end_date or beyond their lifecycle_state = ACTIVE.

- Multiple concurrent delegations from one approver to different delegates are not permitted — only one active delegation per approver at any time.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a delegate becomes inactive mid-window, delegation auto-terminates and routing reverts to the original approver; if the original is also inactive, escalation fires immediately.

**FR-P01-004: Bulk approval**

**Description: **Multiple pending requests of the same type can be approved or rejected in one action. The same audit rules apply per request.

**User story: ***As an Approver, I want to bulk-approve multiple identical requests so that I can process my queue efficiently during peak periods.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Approver can select multiple workflow_instances of the same workflow_id and the same current_stage and submit a single bulk action.

- System validates each instance independently: approver eligibility, request validity, no concurrency conflicts.

- Each instance produces a separate workflow_action record with notes copied from the bulk action input.

- Notifications fire individually per request (one notification per requestor, not a digest).

**Business rules:**

- Rejection requires a rejection reason; if any selected request is rejected, the same rejection reason applies to all in the bulk operation, but the reason field is mandatory at bulk submit.

- Bulk approval scope is limited to 100 requests per single action to bound query and notification load.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If any single instance fails validation, that instance is skipped and reported in the bulk-action summary; other instances complete successfully.

### 5.1.2 P02 — RBAC & Security

**FR-P02-001: Role assignment and Conflict Resolution Rule**

**Description: **Organisation Admin assigns one or more roles to a user. Where a user holds more than one role simultaneously, the more restrictive permission applies. A less permissive role never elevates access granted by a more permissive one.

**User story: ***As an Organisation Admin, I want to assign multiple roles to a single user while ensuring access is always evaluated by the most restrictive permission, so that I can grant operational flexibility without weakening security.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Multiple user_role_assignments records can exist for one user.

- At runtime, when evaluating access to any resource, the system computes the intersection (more restrictive) of all permissions across all of the user’s roles.

- Every role assignment and removal is logged in security_audit_log with event_type = RBAC_CHANGE.

- Time-bound assignments (expires_at NOT NULL) are auto-removed by a scheduled job at expiry.

**Business rules:**

- PII Protection Ceiling overrides all role permissions. No role or combination of roles can override a PII ceiling upward. These ceilings are platform-set configuration (not hard-coded at any level); Organisation Admin may tighten them downward at group or entity level but can never widen PII exposure above the platform floor.

- Office Admin’s national ID and DOB grants are platform-level fixed grants — they survive even the Conflict Resolution Rule because they are not ‘less permissive’ grants of an existing role.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a user holds both an Office Admin role and an Employee role, and their own employee record is accessed, the Office Admin restrictions (no salary, no leave, no performance) apply for their own data on the Office Admin surface; the Employee role surfaces remain accessible separately for own data.

**FR-P02-002: Field-level access enforcement**

**Description: **Every API endpoint validates session token, checks module permission, and checks field-level permission before executing any query. Row-level security is enforced at the database query layer, not the application layer.

**User story: ***As a Platform Security Engineer, I want field-level access enforced at every API call so that a misconfigured front-end cannot expose data that the backend should withhold.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Every API endpoint performs (a) session validation, (b) module-level permission check, (c) field-level permission check against the field policy register, before executing.

- Field-level rules are stored as configuration in the field policy register; new fields default to most restrictive access until explicitly granted.

- Database queries injected via row-level security policies automatically filter rows by tenant_id, entity_id, and the user’s data scope.

- No endpoint is publicly accessible; every endpoint requires a valid authenticated session.

**Business rules:**

- Sensitive PII fields (PAN, Aadhaar, Bank account) require either masking or HR Admin approval workflow to view in unmasked form.

- Performance ratings in draft state cannot be exposed by any entitlement grant — Reviewer-only access is platform-enforced.

- Field-level access change events are logged with previous rule and new rule per the access control audit (P05).

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Attempts to query a field hidden for the requestor’s role return HTTP 403 Forbidden with error code FIELD_ACCESS_DENIED — the field is not silently nulled, because that would create ambiguity with legitimately null values.

**FR-P02-003: MFA enforcement**

**Description: **Multi-Factor Authentication is enforced by default for HR Admin and Organisation Admin roles. TOTP (authenticator app) and SMS OTP are supported methods.

**User story: ***As an HR Admin, I want MFA enforced on my account so that even if my primary credential is compromised, an attacker cannot access HR systems.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- On login, users with role hr_admin or org_admin are required to complete MFA before session activation.

- Supported methods: TOTP (RFC 6238) and SMS OTP.

- MFA enrolment is mandatory at first login for these roles — login cannot complete without enrolment.

- MFA bypass requires Organisation Admin approval and is logged as a security event.

**Business rules:**

- MFA enforcement is configurable per role by Organisation Admin for other roles. HR Admin and Org Admin enforcement is platform-locked at TRUE.

- Successful and failed MFA attempts are logged in security_audit_log with event types MFA_SUCCESS and MFA_FAILURE.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a user loses access to their MFA device, Organisation Admin can initiate an MFA reset which requires identity verification offline; the reset is logged as a security event.

**FR-P02-004: Account lockout policy**

**Description: **Configurable failed-attempt threshold and lockout duration. All lockout events logged.

**User story: ***As an Organisation Admin, I want a configurable lockout policy so that brute-force attempts on credentials are throttled.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Failed login attempts increment users.failed_login_count.

- When failed_login_count reaches the configured threshold (default 5), users.locked_until is set to now() + configured lockout duration (default 30 minutes), and users.status is set to LOCKED.

- All ACCOUNT_LOCKED events are logged with timestamp, IP, and user agent.

- Successful login resets failed_login_count to 0.

**Business rules:**

- Lockout threshold and duration configurable by Organisation Admin per tenant.

- IP restriction can also be enabled by Org Admin — login attempts from outside the allowed IP range are blocked at the API gateway and logged separately.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Lockout from the same IP across multiple accounts (potential credential-stuffing) is surfaced on a security dashboard for Organisation Admin to investigate.

### 5.1.3 P03 — Chat Agent (AI)

**FR-P03-001: Document-grounded policy Q****&****A**

**Description: **Chat Agent answers queries only from two grounding sources: (a) organisation-uploaded HR policy and statutory documents (via M11), (b) platform-managed help guide documents. If the answer is not in these sources, the Chat Agent states so explicitly.

**User story:** ****As an Employee, I want to ask questions about my organisation***’****s HR policies in natural language so that I can get immediate, accurate answers without searching policy documents manually.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Chat Agent retrieves relevant document passages from grounding sources and constructs a prompt for the AI model.

- If no relevant passage is found, Chat Agent responds with an explicit ‘I don’t have information about that in your organisation’s policies or my help guides’ message.

- Every query is logged in ai_chat_queries with metadata only — query content and response content are not logged for privacy.

- Chat Agent is accessible from a persistent icon on all screens (global element).

**Business rules:**

- All AI API calls are made from the backend — never from the browser. API key is never exposed to the client.

- PII is never sent to the AI model — all context is stripped of personal identifiers server-side before the API call is made.

- PII stripping mechanism is governed by the same PII protection ceilings — fields classified as government identifiers, financial, biometric, or health data are excluded from AI context regardless of the querying role’s access level.

- AI-generated outputs that inform HR decisions are informational only — they do not trigger automated actions or workflow approvals without human confirmation.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If the AI API is unavailable, Chat Agent surfaces a graceful failure message and the platform continues to function normally — Chat Agent is additive, not load-bearing.

- Chat Agent response target latency is < 8 seconds end-to-end including the AI API call; if exceeded, an interim ‘still thinking’ indicator is shown.

**FR-P03-002: Pre-joining Chat Agent access**

**Description: **Candidates in pre-joining state can access Chat Agent for policy and process queries. No live HRMS data is accessible to candidates.

**User story: ***As a Pre-joiner, I want to ask the Chat Agent about company policies and what to expect before my joining date so that I am prepared on Day 1.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Pre-joiners’ Chat Agent has access to the same two grounding sources: organisation-uploaded policy documents and platform help guides.

- Pre-joiners cannot query for live HRMS data (e.g. cannot ask ‘who is my manager?’ since no employee record exists yet).

- All pre-joining queries logged with user_role = ‘candidate’.

**Business rules:**

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a candidate’s pre-joining portal access is revoked (offer withdrawn), their Chat Agent access terminates at the same moment.

**FR-P03-003: Process guidance Chat Agent**

**Description: **Chat Agent walks employees step-by-step through HRMS workflows — how to apply for leave, submit attendance regularisation, raise a separation request, initiate goal plan, complete self-review, raise a grievance — grounded on platform-managed help guide documents.

**User story: ***As an Employee unfamiliar with HRMS workflows, I want the Chat Agent to walk me through any process step-by-step in natural language so that I can complete the task without searching documentation or filing a helpdesk ticket.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Process guidance corpus is a separate set of platform-managed help guide documents distinct from organisation-uploaded policy documents (M11).

- Help guides are versioned per platform release and updated atomically — Chat Agent always references the version live in the user’s tenant.

- Chat Agent recognises process-guidance intents (verbs like “how do I”, “walk me through”, “steps to”) and prioritises help-guide retrieval; falls back to policy retrieval if no help-guide match.

- Chat Agent surfaces deep-link buttons within step-by-step responses (e.g. “Take me there” → routes to /apply-leave) when the process maps to a known route.

- Available to all active employee roles and Candidates in pre-joining state, identical RBAC envelope as FR-P03-001.

**Business rules:**

- Same governance ceilings as FR-P03-001: backend-only API calls, PII stripping pre-prompt, full audit logging of metadata only, document-grounded answers only.

- AI-generated step-by-step instructions are informational — they do not pre-fill or auto-submit forms on behalf of the user.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If the user’s role does not have access to the suggested workflow (e.g. Employee asks “how do I run payroll?”), Chat Agent states the action is not available to the user’s role and offers a relevant alternative.

- If the help-guide library is loading or temporarily unavailable, Chat Agent falls back to policy retrieval and flags reduced capability.

**FR-P03-004: Insurance ****&**** benefits Chat Agent**

**Description: **Chat Agent answers group insurance and benefits queries (cashless network, claim process, enrolment windows, dependant coverage, top-up options) grounded on M11-uploaded insurance and benefits policy documents.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want to ask about my group health insurance — what’s covered, how to claim, who I can add as a dependant — and get accurate answers without calling HR or the TPA.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Insurance & benefits documents uploaded via M11 are tagged with document_category = INSURANCE_BENEFITS in M11.

- Chat Agent recognises insurance-related intents (mentions of insurance, claim, cashless, hospital, dependant, premium, enrolment) and routes retrieval to the INSURANCE_BENEFITS document set first.

- Same accessibility envelope as FR-P03-001 — all active employee roles plus pre-joiners (for pre-enrolment queries).

- Phase 1: insurance corpus available via M11 policy upload; Phase 3: tighter integration with M14 Benefits & Insurance module (enrolment windows, real-time claim status) is out of scope.

**Business rules:**

- Phase 1 governance ceilings apply identically to FR-P03-001.

- For claim-specific queries that require live claim status, Chat Agent states the limitation and directs the user to the TPA helpline or HR Admin.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If no insurance documents are uploaded for the tenant, Chat Agent responds “Your organisation has not uploaded insurance documents yet — please contact HR”.

**FR-P03-005: Leadership AI Chat Agent — restricted access**

**Description: **A privileged AI Chat Agent for HR Admin and CEO roles only that answers leadership and PMO questions across project allocation, bench/availability, skill-based search, internal mobility, natural-language analytics, leadership briefing generation, and predictive headcount forecasting. Implements the Section 5 use cases from the AI Use Cases Prioritisation document. Restricted by role.

**User story: ***As an HR Admin or CEO, I want a single AI assistant that answers cross-cutting leadership questions (“which engineers are on notice on the Acme project?”, “show me Python + AWS engineers available from June”, “draft this week’s leadership briefing”) without me assembling spreadsheets from 4 modules.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Access strictly limited to users with role_code IN (hr_admin, ceo). Any other role attempting to access the Leadership AI endpoint receives HTTP 403 and the access attempt is logged.

- Leadership AI Chat Agent is rendered as a separate icon/entry point distinct from the standard Policy Chat Agent — visible only to authorised roles. Standard Policy Chat Agent (FR-P03-001 / 003 / 004) remains accessible to these roles for ordinary policy queries.

- Eight leadership use cases supported in Phase 1.5 / Phase 2 (priority per the AI Use Cases Prioritisation matrix):

- **L1 — Project risk: notice-period engineers view** (P0) — surfaces engineers on notice grouped by project + client with LWD countdown.

- **L2 — Project movement tracker** (P1) — engineers moving IN/OUT of projects over rolling 30/60/90 day windows.

- **L3 — Skill-based engineer search** (P1) — natural-language search across M01 skills/resume/education with availability date.

- **L4 — Bench / availability search** (P1) — unallocated engineers, rolling-off in next N days, under-utilised allocations.

- **L5 — Internal mobility / IJP match** (P2) — open M08 requisitions matched to internal candidates by skill + performance + rollover date.

- **L6 — Natural-language analytics query** (P2) — text-to-query over M16 aggregates with strict RBAC enforcement at row level.

- **L7 — Leadership briefing generator** (P2) — weekly/monthly prose summary synthesising headcount, attrition, notice pipeline, bench utilisation, hiring funnel.

- **L8 — Predictive headcount ****&**** hiring demand forecast** (P3) — headcount gap by skill/department over next 1–2 quarters.

- Each leadership query is logged with metadata (user_id, role, intent, latency, token usage); query content and response content are not logged.

**Business rules:**

- All governance ceilings from FR-P03-001 apply identically PLUS the following extras for Section 5 capabilities:

- **RBAC at retrieval layer.** RBAC row-level and field-level rules apply at the retrieval layer BEFORE any prompt is built. CEO sees the entity; HR Admin sees the entity excluding their own record per separation of duties. Sensitive fields (compensation, BGV status, performance ratings in calibration draft) are NEVER passed to the AI model regardless of role.

- **No LLM-generated numbers.** For analytics queries (L6, L7), all figures must come from M16 aggregates. The LLM may compose narrative around the numbers but must NEVER produce them. Outputs include the M16 query reference for audit.

- **Sandboxed query execution** for L6 (text-to-query). Generated queries execute in a read-only sandbox with row-level RBAC enforced at the database layer, not the application layer. SQL injection and over-broad retrieval blocked at the sandbox boundary.

- **DPDPA and Legal review required** before Phase 1.5 GA for L5, L7, L8 — these surface combined skill + performance + compensation signals and require explicit consent governance + retention policy.

- Leadership AI does NOT auto-take action — no requisition creation, no internal transfer initiation, no headcount adjustment. Outputs are informational; downstream actions are taken via the relevant module (M08 for requisitions, M01 for transfers).

- Audit log for leadership queries retains the user_id, role, query intent classification (L1–L8), tokens consumed, response latency, and timestamp. Query and response content are never logged per FR-P03-001 governance.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- HR Admin role transitions to non-HR Admin (e.g. role change): Leadership AI access revokes within 1 minute of role change committed to the database.

- CEO is a single-entity role per tenant. If CEO record is in NOTICE or LAST_WORKING_DAY lifecycle state (FR-M01), Leadership AI access continues until LWD but is flagged in audit.

- If M01 project_allocated field is null for a target population (early-tenancy where allocation data has not been backfilled), L1–L4 use cases return “No project allocation data available — populate M01 §F.1 Work Roles before using project queries”.

- Hallucination guard for L6 (text-to-query): if generated SQL fails sandbox validation (over-broad, references unauthorised tables, missing RBAC filter), the query is rejected and Chat Agent responds with “I couldn’t run that query safely — try rephrasing or use M16 Custom Report Builder directly”.

- Network failures: Leadership AI fails closed, not open. If sandbox or RBAC service is unavailable, queries are rejected — no fallback to unrestricted queries.

### 5.1.3.6 Phase 3 — Exhaustive AI Journey (Chat, Voice, Teams)

*Phase 3 scope — forward-looking; not a Phase 1 deliverable and not a dependency for Phase 1 or Phase 2.*

**Description:** Phase 3 evolves the document-grounded Chat Agent (FR-P03-001 to FR-P03-005) into an exhaustive, cross-module AI layer delivered across three interaction surfaces — a conversational chat interface, a voice-based assistant, and Microsoft Teams — all sharing the same grounding and governance.

**Chat:** The in-app Chat Agent extends to cross-module assistance — recruitment screening, attendance and leave anomaly detection, performance and goal-drafting support, document classification, and predictive insights such as attrition risk — within each user's access scope. An agentic assistant can guide and pre-fill multi-step workflows, always returning to a human for confirmation before any state change.

**Voice:** A voice-based assistant accepts spoken queries for the same capabilities. Audio is transcribed and processed server-side, captured audio is not retained beyond the transaction, and the spoken or on-screen response is drawn only from data within the querying user's access scope.

**Teams:** Microsoft Teams becomes an additional delivery surface — the Chat Agent is exposed as a Teams bot, workflow approvals are delivered as actionable Teams cards, and notifications and SLA escalations route to Teams, with single sign-on carrying the user's identity and access scope. Teams is an additive channel, never the system of record.

**Governance (all three surfaces):** Every capability inherits the Phase 1 AI governance unchanged — backend-only AI calls (the API key is never exposed to the client), server-side PII stripping under the §2.5 / §3.2 protection ceilings, human confirmation before any state-changing action (no AI capability triggers an automated approval or irreversible action on its own), and full audit logging of model, role, query type, and token usage, with query and response content not logged. The same rules apply identically to typed, spoken, and Teams-delivered interaction.

**Phase boundary:** Phase 1 ships only the document-grounded Chat Agent in FR-P03-001 to FR-P03-005; voice, Teams, and cross-module AI are Phase 3 and are not built in Phase 1.

### 5.1.4 P04 — Tenant & Org Admin

**FR-P04-001: Tenant provisioning**

**Description: **A new tenant can be provisioned and operational within one business day. Provisioning loads segment and geography defaults, creates the initial Organisation Admin user, and applies platform master data.

**User story: ***As a Platform Super Admin, I want to provision a new tenant by selecting segment, geography, and deployment model so that the customer is ready to begin configuration on the same day.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Provisioning creates a tenants row with all required configuration fields populated.

- Segment and geography selection cascades platform defaults: leave policies, performance framework, attendance model, payroll components, workflow templates, analytics dashboards, tax rules, social security rates, labour law defaults, holiday calendar.

- Initial Organisation Admin user is created and emailed login credentials with a forced password change on first login (or directed to Google SSO).

- Provisioning audit logged with all configuration choices.

**Business rules:**

- Segment cannot be changed post-provisioning without data migration.

- Geography cannot be changed post-provisioning. Additional geographies can be added per entity at any time.

- Configuration cascades downward: platform default → tenant default → entity override → employee exception. Changes at a higher level do not automatically override lower-level customisations; they update only the default for future provisioning.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If platform master data for the selected segment+geography combination is incomplete, provisioning is blocked with an actionable error listing missing data items.

- Standalone-to-Multi-Tenant or Standalone-to-Group-Company upgrade is a configuration change only — no data migration required.

**FR-P04-002: Device and location registration**

**Description: **Biometric devices and IP-based attendance restrictions require prior device and location registration in P04 before M05 configuration can be activated.

**User story: ***As an Organisation Admin, I want to register biometric devices and office locations centrally so that downstream attendance configuration in M05 has the required references.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- biometric_devices and locations entities are created in P04 by Org Admin or Platform Super Admin.

- M05 geofence configuration, IP restriction configuration, and biometric device-to-employee mapping all require valid references to P04-registered devices and locations.

- If a device or location is not yet registered, M05 configuration UI displays a clear blocker message referencing P04.

- Device registration includes device_id, location_id, device_type, and health check parameters.

**Business rules:**

- Devices can be marked OFFLINE without deletion — historical attendance records remain intact and queryable.

- Location deactivation requires reassignment of any employees with that location_id in their active employment_history.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a device sends punch records for a biometric_user_id that has no biometric_employee_mapping, the punch is held in a quarantine queue surfaced to the Attendance Admin.

### 5.1.5 P05 — Audit & Compliance Log

**FR-P05-001: Database-trigger-driven immutable audit log**

**Description: **Every INSERT, UPDATE, and soft-DELETE on any table writes an entry to the audit log. The audit log is written by a database trigger — not application code. The audit log table has no UPDATE or DELETE permission granted to any database user, with the sole exception of system-initiated PII redaction under the right-to-erasure workflow.

**User story: ***As a Data Protection Officer, I want every mutation logged immutably by a database trigger so that no application bug, developer action, or API bypass can suppress an audit entry.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- 100% of mutations on all tables captured — zero gaps permitted.

- Each entry records: table, record ID, field name, old value, new value, changed by, timestamp (UTC), IP address, user agent.

- PII fields stored in masked form only in audit entries — raw PII is never written to the audit log.

- Audit log queries are themselves logged — viewing an audit log is an auditable action.

**Business rules:**

- Minimum retention: 7 years. After 2 years, entries may be archived to cold storage but must remain queryable within 24 hours.

- Audit log entries are exportable as structured data for submission to auditors or regulatory authorities.

- Organisation Admin can query security audit log for any role change or login event within their tenant.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Right-to-erasure under DPDPA may overwrite a PII field’s old_value with a redaction marker — this is the only permitted mutation of the audit log, and is itself logged as a system erasure event.

### 5.1.6 P06 — Migration Toolkit

**FR-P06-001: Darwinbox ETL framework**

**Description: **Structured ETL+V pattern (Extract → Validate → Transform → Load → Verify) for Darwinbox migration. Three mandatory dry runs before any production cutover. Every migrated record carries a darwinbox_source_id column.

**User story: ***As a Platform Super Admin, I want a tooled migration pathway from Darwinbox with mandatory dry runs and traceability so that customer cutovers happen with high confidence and** ****<**** **0.5% error rate.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Migration scripts are idempotent — running the same script twice produces the same result.

- Every migrated table carries a darwinbox_source_id column populated on insert; this is the permanent traceability link from source to current state.

- Three mandatory dry runs against a staging environment before any production cutover; production cutover is gated on Dry Run 3 achieving < 0.5% failure rate for Waves 1 and 2.

- Wave 3 (payroll history, Phase 2) requires zero tolerance for numeric discrepancies in payroll amounts.

**Business rules:**

- Wave 1 (runs after Phase 2, once the business is comfortable with the base) covers: employee master, employment types, org structure, leave balances and 2-year history, attendance records (24 months).

- Wave 2 (Phase 1 dependent) covers: performance history (last 4 cycles), job history and promotions, employee contracts, separation records, active recruitment pipeline, holiday calendars.

- Wave 1 must complete ahead of the single post-migration go-live. Wave 2 completes before or concurrent with the post-migration go-live.

- Migration tooling access is restricted to Platform Super Admin (tenant-level operations) and HR Admin (entity-level data verification and field mapping review only). No other role has access to migration scripts, ETL outputs, or staging data.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a migration record fails validation, that record is logged with its source row and the specific validation rule that failed; the wave continues with the remaining records.

- Darwinbox is kept in read-only mode for a minimum of 4 weeks after go-live before the customer’s Darwinbox subscription is cancelled.

## 5.2 M01 — Employee Master

**FR-M01-001: Single employee record per tenant**

**Description: **One employee equals one M01 record across the tenant. Cross-entity transfers create a new employment_history version, not a new record. Rehire after separation creates a new employees row with a link to the prior employee_id via previous_employee_id.

**User story: ***As an HR Admin, I want a single canonical employee record across all entities of the tenant so that group reporting, transfers, and rehires are seamless without duplicate identities.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- employees table has a unique (tenant_id, employee_id) constraint enforced at the database layer.

- Cross-entity transfer creates a new employment_history row with the new entity_id and effective_from = transfer date; the previous row is closed at effective_from − 1.

- Rehire creates a new employees row; previous_employee_id is populated; continuous_service_flag is set if applicable.

- Group date of joining is preserved across transfers and rehires — never changes thereafter.

**Business rules:**

- Employee ID is immutable post-assignment. Cannot be changed by HR Admin, Org Admin, or migration.

- Employee ID format: {entity_short_code}{6-digit zero-padded sequence}. Sequence range may extend to 7 digits at provisioning. Unique within tenant, not just entity.

- ID assigned at the moment of Candidate → Pre-joining transition (M08 emits the event; M01 generates the ID). Not before.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Sequence collisions are blocked at the database layer via unique constraint. Bulk import (P06) collisions cause that record to fail validation with explicit error code; the load continues for other records.

- Cross-entity transfer with an effective_from in the past requires HR Admin justification text captured in change_reason.

**FR-M01-002: Effective-date versioning on employment fields**

**Description: **Every change to a versioned employment field (grade, designation, department, reporting manager, employment type, location, entity, functional area, contribution level, band) writes a new employment_history row with an effective_date. Past-dated and future-dated changes supported.

**User story: ***As an HR Admin, I want effective-date versioning on key employment fields so that I can backfill missed changes or schedule future changes without manual workarounds.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- On any write to a versioned field, a new employment_history row is inserted with effective_from = supplied date (default today); the previously open row (effective_to IS NULL) is closed with effective_to = new effective_from − 1.

- Past-dated changes are inserted at the correct historical position; downstream consumers re-read the corrected version.

- Future-dated changes are staged in the table with effective_from > today. A scheduled job runs daily at 00:00 IST and ‘applies’ the change on the effective date — downstream notifications fire on application, not staging.

- Correction flag set TRUE on past-dated entries to distinguish them from natural versioning.

**Business rules:**

- Personal contact fields (email, mobile, address) are not versioned — they overwrite in place with an audit log entry.

- If an employee transitions to Notice state before a staged future-dated change’s effective date, the staged change is automatically cancelled. HR Admin sees a summary of cancelled staged changes.

- Effective date on a weekend or holiday is honoured as set — the scheduled job applies the change regardless of working-day status.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Save-time validation rejects a new reporting_manager_id if it would create a cycle in the reporting chain.

- Versioned history retention is indefinite during active state; subject to soft-delete and DPDPA erasure rules.

**FR-M01-003: PII tiered classification and masking**

**Description: **PII fields are classified into four tiers (Tier 1 Restricted, Tier 2 Sensitive, Tier 3 Personal, Directory). Tier 1 fields are encrypted at rest using AES-256-GCM and masked in all display contexts. PII Protection Ceiling — no role can override these ceilings upward.

**User story: ***As a Data Protection Officer, I want PII classification and masking enforced platform-wide so that sensitive data is not exposed even to administrators outside the explicit Office Admin grants.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- PAN, Aadhaar, passport, bank account, salary/CTC are Tier 1 — encrypted at rest, masked in all display contexts (PAN as XXXXX1234X, Aadhaar as XXXX-XXXX-1234, bank as ****1234).

- Every PII view event for Tier 1 is logged in security_audit_log with viewer, field, employee, timestamp, IP.

- Office Admin national ID grants (PAN, Aadhaar, Passport, Driving Licence) and DOB grant are platform-level grants — fixed and not configurable upward or downward.

- Tier 1 access cannot be granted to any role via individual entitlement beyond the platform-defined ceilings.

**Business rules:**

- DPDPA consent is captured at employee onboarding. Consent records are immutable — they can be superseded but never deleted.

- Employees can view their own consent history through self-service. Consent withdrawal triggers data erasure workflow for non-statutory data.

- Right of access: employee can export own record with PII unmasked (employee owns own data).

- Right of erasure: subject to statutory retention; non-statutory PII deleted; statutory data archived but never deleted.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Where non-statutory PII exists in immutable audit log (as old field values), it is overwritten with a redaction marker at erasure time — the audit entry structure is preserved but the PII value is replaced. This is the only permitted mutation of the audit log and is itself logged as a system erasure event.

- Encryption keys are rotatable without re-encrypting existing data (envelope encryption). All key rotation events logged.

**FR-M01-004: Lifecycle state machine**

**Description: **Every employee record exists in one of seven states: Pre-joining, Active, Notice, Last Working Day, Separated, Absconded, Rehire Eligible. Probation is a flag (probation_flag + probation_end_date) on the employment record, not a peer state — an employee in probation is in state Active with probation_flag = TRUE. State transitions are governed by workflow events from other modules — no manual state change without an audit log entry. Rehire Eligible is entered from Separated on a good-standing exit disposition (auto from the exit reason or an explicit HR Admin mark) and exits to a new employee record on rehire via M08 (previous_employee_id linked, group date of joining preserved); it may revert to Separated if eligibility is revoked on a post-exit finding.

**User story: ***As an HR Admin, I want lifecycle states automatically managed through workflow events so that access rights, workflow eligibility, and system visibility align consistently with employment status.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- lifecycle_state column on employees is updated only by the designated trigger module (M02 for Pre-joining→Active, M03 for Active→Notice→LWD→Separated, M08 for Candidate→Pre-joining, M05 for Active→Absconded).

- Each transition event creates an audit log entry capturing the previous state, new state, triggering module, and triggering user.

- Probation is tracked as a flag on employment_history (probation_flag), not a separate lifecycle_state — employees on probation remain in ACTIVE state with full system access.

- Joining date is not the automatic activation trigger — HR Admin explicitly initiating onboarding (M02) is the trigger.

**Business rules:**

- Notice state: employees cannot initiate new leave requests above threshold per leave type policy. Reduced workflow initiation rights.

- Separated state: read-only access to own payslips and documents for configurable period (default 180 days post-LWD).

- Absconded state: HR Admin action required; restricted access; not a terminal state — can transition back to Active via HR Admin action or convert to Separated via M03.

- Rehire after separation creates a new record — not a state transition on the prior record.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If HR Admin attempts to manually set lifecycle_state without a valid workflow event, the change is rejected at the application layer.

- Staged future-dated employment changes are auto-cancelled when employee transitions to Notice state.

**FR-M01-020: Employee detail view — role-based access tiers**

**Description: **The employee detail screen (full profile across all M01 clusters) is exposed with three access tiers, enforced by role.

**Acceptance criteria:**

- **Full add / view / edit** — **HR Admin only**. HR Admin can view all fields unmasked (Tier-1 IDs included), edit any field (Tier-1 edits route through the L2 sensitive-change workflow per DM12), and create new employee records via the Add Employee flow (FR-M01-001).

- **Read-only scoped view** — **HRBP, HOD, CEO**. These roles open the same detail screen in read-only mode: no edit controls, no Add Employee action, and Tier-1 identifiers (Aadhaar, PAN, bank) are masked. Visible scope follows each role’s existing data entitlement (HRBP: assigned employees; HOD: department / UAG; CEO: entity). Every view is audit-logged.

- **Entitlement-scoped self / manager views** — **Employee** sees and edits their own profile per the self-service field rules; **L1 Manager** sees the manager-scoped reportee view (FR-M01-005). These are unchanged.

- All tiers render from one shared detail definition; the access tier is resolved at render time from the viewer’s role, and no tier can elevate a role’s underlying field-level or row-level entitlement (the §3.9 ceilings still apply).

**Business rules:**

- The read-only tier never exposes unmasked Tier-1 PII regardless of the broader role; only the full tier (HR Admin) and the platform-granted Office Admin national-ID exception see unmasked national IDs.

- Add Employee is restricted to HR Admin only. Performance Admin holds no M01 employee-master tier; it accesses employee context through M09 surfaces (calibration moderation, review status) and retains all §3.7.5 performance permissions.

**FR-M01-005: Org chart with group-wide visibility**

**Description: **Org chart is stored as a parent-pointer tree via reporting_manager_id on employment_history (versioned). Group Company deployment spans all entities within the tenant — employees can see the full group structure. Drill-down respects PII rules.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want to navigate the organisation chart for my entire group so that I understand the structure and can identify the right person to engage with.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- getReportingChain(employee_id, direction=up|down, depth=1..N|full) returns active employees only by default. Separated, Pre-joining, and Absconded employees are excluded.

- getOrgChartNode(employee_id) returns directory-tier fields only — name, designation, department, official email, official mobile, office location, profile photo. No PII or compensation.

- Up to 4 levels render p95 < 1500 ms. Beyond 4 levels: lazy-loaded on user expand action.

- Group Company org chart spans all entities; entity-scoped queries available via getReportingChain(employee_id, scope=entity).

**Business rules:**

- Reporting chain queries on a separated employee return their former reports as ‘orphaned’ — surface the next active manager up the chain.

- Org chart excludes employees in Separated, Absconding, and Pre-joining states from the visible tree.

- Notice and LWD employees are rendered with a state badge visible to HR Admin and manager hierarchy only.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Cycle prevention enforced at save: setting reporting_manager_id is rejected if it would create a cycle in the reporting chain.

- Data drift (a cycle detected post-write, e.g. through bulk update) is audit-flagged and surfaces in the Organisation Admin’s exception queue.

**FR-M01-006: Bulk upload — initial tenant load**

**Description: **P06 Migration Toolkit calls M01 bulk-load contract during Wave 1. Each input row carries a darwinbox_source_id persisted on the M01 record. Failure threshold < 0.5% per Product Vision §3.8.

**User story: ***As a Platform Super Admin executing Wave 1 migration, I want a bulk-load contract that applies the full field policy and reports per-record success/failure so that I can converge on the** ****<**** **0.5% target across dry runs.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Bulk upload accepts CSV or JSON with one row per employee.

- Field policy applied per the field register: mandatory fields enforced, sensitive fields routed appropriately, versioned fields written as history rows.

- Dry-run mandatory before live load — produces a per-record validation report without committing.

- Throughput target: 10,000 records / hour for bulk-load.

**Business rules:**

- Each bulk operation produces one parent audit event (operation, initiator, file hash, row count) and per-record child events (record updated / skipped / failed).

- All bulk operations are idempotent on darwinbox_source_id or operation_request_id — running the same operation twice produces the same result; duplicate rows are skipped and reported.

- Sensitive fields (PAN, Aadhaar, Bank) cannot be bulk-updated post-migration — they require the per-record approval flow.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a record’s PAN already exists for a different employee_id (uniqueness collision), the record fails validation with explicit error code; load continues for other records.

- Validation report can be exported and resubmitted with corrections; idempotency ensures already-loaded records are skipped on rerun.

**FR-M01-007: Profile completeness gating**

**Description: **Emergency contact (name, relation, mobile) is the platform-locked mandatory minimum. Leave application (M04) and expense submission are blocked if mandatory minimum is incomplete.

**User story: ***As a People Operations Lead, I want incomplete employee profiles to be blocked from key actions so that employees are nudged to complete data essential for HR operations.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- profile_completeness_pct on employees is computed from the configured mandatory field set (platform minimum + HR-Admin additions).

- Leave application API rejects submission if the employee’s profile is below the mandatory minimum, with a clear message indicating the missing fields.

- Profile-completion banner appears on the dashboard and profile for employees below 90% completeness. Banner dismissable but reappears weekly until threshold met.

- Aggregated completeness scores feed M16 analytics for HR oversight.

**Business rules:**

- Platform-locked minimum is at least one emergency_contacts row with name, relation, and mobile.

- HR Admin can add fields to the mandatory set per tenant but cannot remove the platform-locked minimum.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- On rehire of a previously separated employee, profile completeness is computed against the current mandatory set — not against historical data.

**FR-M01-008: Dependent and nominee records**

**Description: **Dependent records capture relation, name, DOB, gender, dependent classes (Insurance, Gratuity, PF, EPS, ESI), and nominee percentages per class. Sum of nominee_percentage = 100 per class is validated at save. Max 10 dependents per employee (tenant-configurable).

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want to record my dependents and nominees during onboarding so that statutory payouts (gratuity, PF) and benefits (insurance) are configured correctly from Day 1.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Dependents captured during M02 Onboarding benefits intent collection. Employee can self-service edit thereafter.

- Validation: name 1–120 chars, relation enum, DOB ≤ today, sum of nominee_percentage = 100 per class.

- Non-statutory edits: direct save. Initial statutory nominee capture by employee during M02 onboarding: direct save (employee naming own nominee for first time).

- Subsequent changes to statutory nominee (gratuity, PF, ESI) after initial save: HR Admin approval workflow.

**Business rules:**

- Gratuity nominee: data captured during M02 (Phase 1); mandatory at probation confirmation. Operationally consumed by M07 gratuity payout and M06 FnF death-in-service payout (both Phase 2).

- PF nominee: data captured during M02 (Phase 1); mandatory if employee is PF-eligible. Operationally consumed by M07 PF enrolment and ECR challan (Phase 2).

- Insurance dependents: captured at M02 as preference intent only; operationally consumed by M14 (Phase 3).

- Navigation placement: in addition to the combined Add Dependent form, a dedicated Nominees screen is provided under the Employee Profile that lists nominees with their class allocations and enforces the per-class 100% rule on save. It supports view, edit, and remove of nominee allocations without re-opening the full dependent record; statutory-class changes (Gratuity, PF, ESI) still route through HR Admin approval per the rules above.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If nominee_percentage sum ≠ 100 for any class, save is rejected with the specific class identified in the error message.

- Statutory nominee changes that fail HR Admin approval revert to the prior version; no partial save.

**FR-M01-009: Grievance register**

**Description: **Standalone sub-module of M01. Workplace, Manager, Compensation, Other, and POSH categories. Anonymous opt-in. POSH routes to ICC committee (not HR Admin).

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want to raise a grievance — anonymously if needed — through a structured channel so that workplace concerns are heard and acted on.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Employee submits via Grievance form with fields: category, subject, description, anonymity flag, attachments.

- Anonymous flag opt-in by employee. When set: HR Admin sees category and count only — not identity or content.

- Identity revealed to POSH ICC only for POSH-category grievances.

- Status workflow: Open → Under Investigation → Resolved → Closed. Audit-logged at each transition.

- Navigation placement: grievance raising is accessed from the Employee Profile (the two profile entry points are retained); it is not exposed as a separate left-navigation menu item in any role menu.

**Business rules:**

- Routing: Workplace, Manager, Other, Compensation → HR Admin queue. POSH → POSH ICC committee queue (not HR Admin, even non-anonymous).

- HR Admin sees aggregate POSH count for reporting only.

- HR Admin acknowledgement SLA: 2 business days. Resolution target: 30 business days non-POSH, 90 business days POSH (statutory).

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Grievance content is privileged — only HR Admin and POSH ICC have content access; M16 analytics consumes aggregate counts only, never content or identity.

**FR-M01-010: Resume upload ****&**** versioning**

**Description: **Employees can upload, replace, and view their resume on the Skills tab of the Employee Profile. All historical versions are retained in M11 Document Management vault per DPDPA 7-year retention. Visibility: L1 Manager, HRBP, Recruiter (for IJP), and HR Admin. Other employees cannot view peer resumes.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want to keep my resume up to date on my profile so that my manager and HR see the latest version when considering me for internal opportunities, and so that IJP applications auto-populate without re-uploading.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Accepts PDF and DOCX up to 25 MB per M11 DM06 file-size cap.

- Single “current” resume per employee. Uploading a new version archives the previous version with timestamp.

- Resume metadata (filename, size, upload timestamp) shown on profile. PII-tier 2 — encrypted at rest, access-logged.

- IJP module (M08) reads the current resume from M11 via the employee_resume reference.

**Business rules:**

- Visibility scope: Self (always), L1 Manager (always), HRBP (always for assigned employees), Recruiter (only during active IJP application), HR Admin (always with audit log).

- L2+ managers and HOD must request access via HRBP for a specific business reason — request logged.

- Resume versions cannot be hard-deleted by employee. Soft-delete moves to archived; HR Admin retains restoration rights for 90 days.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Upload > 25 MB → reject with message and link to compression guide.

- Non-PDF/DOCX upload → reject with message listing accepted types.

- If virus scan (M11 DM08) flags the file, upload fails with generic “scan failed” message; file is quarantined, not stored.

**FR-M01-011: HRBP single point of contact**

**Description: **Every employee has a designated HRBP shown on their profile (Employment tab and Org chart tab). HRBP is the routing target for grievances, role-mobility queries, benefits questions, and policy interpretation. HRBP assignment is mapped at department + location level by HR Admin.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want to know who my HRBP is and how to reach them so that I have a single, trusted HR point of contact when I need help.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- HRBP card on Employee Profile shows: HRBP name, photo, email, phone, availability window (e.g. 9 AM – 6 PM IST), and a “Raise grievance” action button.

- HRBP assignment is computed from employees.department_id + employees.location_id via the HRBP mapping table. If multiple mappings match, the most specific one (department + location) wins over broader (department-only).

- HRBP changes (re-assignment) flow through HR Admin with audit log; affected employees notified in-app and via email.

**Business rules:**

- One HRBP per employee at any time. Joint-HRBP arrangements (e.g. transitional handover) not supported in Phase 1.

- HRBP role-holder is an employee with the HRBP capability flag set in M01 (per RBAC §4.3).

- If an employee has no HRBP mapping (orphaned), fallback to the department’s HOD with a system flag for HR Admin attention.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- HRBP on leave (PL/ML/SL ≥ 3 days) → profile shows “Currently on leave — back DD MMM” with a backup HRBP if configured.

- HRBP separated → automatic re-route to department HOD; HR Admin alerted within 1 business day to assign a permanent replacement.

**FR-M01-012: Disability declaration (voluntary)**

**Description: **Employees may voluntarily declare a disability under the Rights of Persons with Disabilities Act 2016. Information used solely for statutory reporting and workplace accommodations. Access restricted to HRBP and HR Admin.

**User story: ***As an Employee with a disability, I want to disclose this confidentially so that PrimeSoft can comply with statutory reporting and provide accommodations I need.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Add Disability form captures: kind of disability, percentage, UDID number (optional), date of certification, disability proof (PDF/image ≤ 25 MB), accommodations needed (free text), and a mandatory consent checkbox.

- Submission routes to HRBP for verification; HR Admin approves before the record is marked Verified.

- Employee can withdraw the disclosure at any time. Withdrawal soft-deletes the record but retains it in audit log per DPDPA.

**Business rules:**

- Disability data is PII tier-1 — encrypted at rest, masked in all views except HRBP and HR Admin, never exposed via M16 Analytics in identified form.

- L1 Manager does not see disability information by default. If accommodations require manager awareness, HRBP communicates only the accommodation, not the underlying disability.

- Statutory reports (annual filing) aggregate by disability category, never identify individuals.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Self-declaration with no UDID → record stored as “Self-declared, unverified”. Workplace accommodations may proceed; statutory filing excludes unverified records.

- Conflicting versions (employee updates while HRBP review pending) → newer version replaces; HRBP notified.

**FR-M01-013: Visa ****&**** bond register**

**Description: **Employees register visas held (B1/B2, H-1B, L-1, Tier 2, Schengen, Employment Pass, etc.) along with associated visa bonds. System fires renewal reminders 90 / 60 / 30 days before expiry.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want to keep my visa details current on my profile so that travel approvals are automatic and renewal reminders fire well before expiry.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Add Visa form captures: country, visa type, visa number, issuing authority, issue date, valid till, multiple entry, max stay per entry, sponsor, visa scan, bond signed flag.

- Multiple visas per employee supported.

- Reminder cron fires 90 / 60 / 30 days before expiry, notifies employee + L1 Manager + HRBP.

- Travel approval workflow (M01 sub-module) reads the active visa register.

**Business rules:**

- Visa scan stored as PII tier-1 (passport-equivalent) in M11 vault.

- “Sponsored by PrimeSoft” visas trigger the visa bond clause — bond document linked from M11.

- Visa expiry within 30 days blocks travel approval submission with override path through HR Admin.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Visa marked expired automatically when expiry_date < current date — record retained in archive, removed from active register.

- Visa renewal in progress → employee can mark “Renewal pending” with target date; reminder cron pauses.

**FR-M01-014: Employee Profile org chart view**

**Description: **Org chart is surfaced as a tab within the Employee Profile (My profile › Org chart tab) showing the employee’s HOD → Skip-level → L1 Manager → Self hierarchy, peers reporting to the same L1 Manager, and the assigned HRBP. Org chart is no longer a separate top-level navigation item.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want to see my place in the org clearly from my own profile so that I know who my manager, skip-level, HOD, HRBP, and peers are without searching the directory.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Org chart tab renders a vertical hierarchy: HOD at top → Skip-level (L2) → L1 Manager → Self (visually highlighted) → Peers (row below Self).

- Each node shows name, role, photo (initials avatar fallback), and is clickable to open that person’s public profile.

- HRBP card shown below the hierarchy with name, role description, contact, and grievance action.

- For HODs and above, Self renders at top of their direct-reports tree (downward view).

**Business rules:**

- Hierarchy computed from employees.manager_id (recursive) and departments.hod_user_id.

- Click on a peer / manager opens their **public** profile — Aadhaar, PAN, salary, and other PII tier-1/2 fields are masked per FR-M01-008 sensitive-field access rules.

- Peers list limited to direct reports of the same L1 Manager (max 50 shown; “View all” link for larger spans).

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Orphaned employee (no manager_id) → org chart shows “Manager assignment pending — contact HR”.

**FR-M01-015: HR letters issued register**

**Description: **Every HR letter issued to an employee (appointment, confirmation, hike/increment, promotion, reward, bond, experience) is filed on the Employee Profile › HR letters tab with reference number, issuer, issue date, and a downloadable PDF. Letters are generated from M11 letter templates and carry an embedded digital signature.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want every official letter HR has issued to me filed in one place so that I can download proof for visa applications, loans, or personal records without raising a ticket.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- HR letters tab displays a chronological table: letter type, issued by, issued on, reference number, download action.

- Letter types in Phase 1: Appointment, Confirmation, Hike/Increment (per FY), Promotion, Reward / spot-bonus, Bond / declaration, Experience (on separation).

- Each letter rendered from an M11 template (FR-M11-002) with merge fields populated from M01 and M06 (compensation) sources at generation time.

- Letters carry embedded HR Admin or HOD digital signature per M11 DM12.

**Business rules:**

- Latest letter of each type is flagged with a “Latest” pill. Historical letters are retained and viewable.

- Employee can request letters not yet issued: Salary certificate, Employment letter (visa/loan), Experience letter, Address-proof letter. Request routes to HR Admin queue with SLA per template (2–5 business days).

- Letters are PII tier-1. Visibility: Self always; L1 Manager, HRBP, HR Admin per RBAC.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Letter request for a type not eligible (e.g. Experience letter while still active) → request rejected with explanation.

- Re-issue request → HR Admin can re-generate from the same template; both old and new versions retained.

**FR-M01-016: Official documents register**

**Description: **Profile › Official documents tab catalogues every signed, declared, or nominated PrimeSoft document for the employee: NDA, NDA Annexure, Client NDA(s), Gratuity Declaration, PF Declaration (Form 11), Insurance Nominations (Personal Accident, Life Insurance), Signed Bond (if applicable), Joining Report, Letter of Authorization. Sourced from M11 document clusters.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want to see every official document I’ve signed with PrimeSoft so that I have a complete record without contacting HR for routine queries.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Official documents tab displays documents grouped by cluster: Sign-off (NDA family), Statutory (Gratuity, PF Form 11), Benefits (Insurance nominations), Compliance (Bond, Letter of Authorization), Onboarding (Joining Report).

- Each document shows: document name, cluster, status (Signed / Filed / N/A), signed on date, reference number, View action.

- Client NDAs are per-project — multiple entries possible; lists active vs archived.

- “Signed Bond” shows N/A status for employees not subject to bond (e.g. senior hires).

**Business rules:**

- Documents are PII tier-1. Encrypted at rest, access-logged, 7-year retention per DPDPA (per FR-M11-003). Published policy documents are retained indefinitely while published and for 10 years post-archival.

- View action surfaces the PDF in a watermarked viewer; download generates a fresh watermarked copy.

- Visibility: Self always; L1 Manager (Joining Report only); HRBP & HR Admin (full set).

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Document signed externally (paper-based) and scanned in → flagged with “Scanned original” indicator.

- Missing required document (e.g. PF Form 11 not signed) → flagged on dashboard for HR Admin and on profile for employee with “Action needed” pill.

**FR-M01-017: Add dependent — expanded data model**

**Description: **Add Dependent form expanded to capture full identity, contact, and benefits scope: First / Middle / Last name, Relation, DOB, Gender, Nationality, Aadhaar (optional, masked storage), Phone, Address, Group medical insurance flag, Nominee flag with share percentage and nominee-for multi-select (PF / Gratuity / Insurance variants), Tax filing joint flag (spouse only).

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want to maintain a complete dependent record once so that group insurance enrolment, statutory nominations, and joint tax filing all draw from the same source.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Mandatory fields: First name, Last name, Relation, DOB.

- Optional fields: Middle name, Gender, Nationality (with country picker datalist), Aadhaar (stored masked, full value access logged to HR Admin), Phone, Address (free text).

- Benefits section: Add to group medical insurance (toggle); Add as nominee (toggle) with conditional Share % and Nominee-for multi-select (PF, Gratuity, Insurance — Group Medical / Personal Accident / Life); Tax filing — joint with this dependent (spouse only).

- Auto-saved as draft on field blur. Save button finalises.

**Business rules:**

- Nominee share per benefit class must sum to 100% across all nominees (per FR-M01-008 statutory rule). Save rejected if class sum ≠ 100% at the time of finalisation.

- Joint tax filing flag visible only when Relation = Spouse and Marital status = Married.

- Aadhaar field optional but if provided, validated against Aadhaar number checksum (Verhoeff algorithm).

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Duplicate dependent (same name + relation + DOB) → warn before save.

- Removing a dependent who is a nominee → require nominee re-allocation before allowing removal.

**FR-M01-018: Add visa — expanded data model**

**Description: **Add Visa form captures Country, Visa type, Visa number, Issuing authority, Issue date, Valid till, Multiple entry flag, Maximum stay per entry (days), Visa scan upload, Sponsor (PrimeSoft / Self / External), Visa bond signed flag.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want to register every visa I hold with full details so that travel approvals are automatic, renewal reminders fire on time, and statutory visa-bond linkage is clear.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Mandatory fields: Country, Visa type (dropdown: B1, B2, H-1B, L-1, Tier 2 General, Schengen Short-stay, Employment Pass, Dependent, Other), Visa number, Issue date, Valid till.

- Optional fields: Issuing authority, Multiple entry (Yes/No), Max stay (days), Visa scan (PDF/image ≤ 25 MB), Sponsor, Bond signed flag.

- Multiple visas per employee supported.

- Renewal reminder cron fires 90 / 60 / 30 days before expiry_date.

**Business rules:**

- “Sponsored by PrimeSoft” auto-links the visa bond document from M11 if previously signed.

- Visa scan is PII tier-1, encrypted at rest, access restricted to Self, HRBP, HR Admin.

- Travel approval (M01 sub-module) blocks submission if no active visa for destination country.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Visa marked active but expiry_date < today → auto-flagged Expired on dashboard.

- Renewal in progress → employee can mark “Renewal pending” with target date; expiry reminders pause until new dates confirmed.

**FR-M01-019: Statutory ****&**** billing fields on profile**

**Description: **PF · EPS · ESIC tab and Gratuity · Insurance · LWF tab on Employee Profile capture the full statutory and project-billing field set: UAN, PF number, PF applicability, PF applicable from, PF registration location, PF restriction amount, Restrict company PF flag, EPS applicability, Pension number, Contribution capped, ESIC applicability, ESIC number, Name as per ESIC, ESIC applicable from, ESIC registration code, Gratuity number, Form F filed flag, Form F document link, LWF applicability, LWF state, LWF designation, LWF rate, Professional Tax, PT slab, TDS applicability, Tax filing joint, Social Security (overseas), Billing status (Billed / Not billed / N/A), Project allocation.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want a single, accurate view of every statutory and benefit field that applies to me so that I can spot errors and raise corrections before they affect payroll or tax filing.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- PF · EPS · ESIC tab displays the PF, EPS, and ESIC field groups in three labelled sections.

- Gratuity · Insurance · LWF tab displays Gratuity, Insurance, LWF, Professional Tax & TDS, and Billing & project allocation field groups.

- Field-level access per FR-M01-008: PF, ESIC, Gratuity, LWF, PT visible to Self & HR Admin always; L1 Manager sees only applicability flags (not amounts); Finance Admin sees full set.

- Billing rate is masked from L1 Manager and from Self; visible to Finance Admin only.

**Business rules:**

- PF restriction amount capped at the statutory ceiling (₹15,000 basic as of current EPFO rule); changes to the ceiling are platform-wide config (Org Admin).

**M09 Performance Management appraisal lifecycle:**

- FR-M09-015 Goal creation — flexible authorship with L1 edit-and-approve. Employee or L1 Manager can create; previous goals can be copied and modified; L1 has Approve / Reject / Sendback / Edit-and-Approve; employee notified on L1 edits.

- FR-M09-016 Sendback discards L1’s prior evaluation..

- FR-M09-019 **Goal score computation.** Per-goal formula: goal_score = min(goal_achievement_pct, 120) / 100 × goal_weightage. Overall: sum(goal_score) / 100. 120% achievement cap for scoring; raw % displayed. Sub-goals informational only — parent achievement manual. Sub-goal weightages sum to 100% within parent. **Plan-level weightage sum = 100% hard-enforced at submission AND approval — no role can bypass.** Resolves PN-PF02 default.

- FR-M09-021 SLA settings & escalation. Editable by HR Admin OR Performance Admin. Defaults: Self review 5d · Evaluation 7d · Calibration 10d · Acknowledgement 5d · Approval requests 3d..

- FR-M09-022 Manual calibration and publish. HR Admin OR Performance Admin (joint capability). No automatic publish — publish is explicit, irreversible without un-publish.

- FR-M09-023 Calibration ideal distribution norm. Default bell curve 15% top / 70% middle / 15% bottom. HRBP per-cycle override audit-logged. Benchmark only, not forced quota..

- FR-M09-024 Cycle closure. Acknowledgement SLA 5 business days; auto-ack on SLA breach; goals close (become read-only) when the cycle closes; mid-cycle separation handling; dispute path within 5 business days post-ack (HRBP → L2 → HR Admin)..

- Out of scope for Phase 1 (deferred or ignored per business decision): goal cascade (FR-M09-014 in FS stands; no new FR), check-in cadence, bulk goal assignment, framework administrative settings (character limits, landing URL, grid sizes).

- §4.10.2 goals entity gains the goal_measurement field (mandatory text, ≥ 20 chars, captures KPI/formula/data source/threshold). Measurement criteria are captured as free text — there is no structured Metric entity in Phase 1. Measurement criteria surfaced alongside goal description on the appraisal review screen.

- FR-M09-015 acceptance criteria expanded: **Copy from previous goal** scope distinguished — Employee picker shows only own prior goals; L1 Manager picker shows any current team member’s prior goals (filter by reportee + pillar + cycle; max 50 results); goal_measurement included in pre-populated copy fields; goals_history.copied_from_goal_id tracks lineage.

- FR-M09-019 plan weightage rule strengthened to **hard enforcement, no bypass**. Submit and approval both block when sum(goal_weightage) != 100. Calibration consistency preserved.

- **FR-M09-025 L1 Manager appraisal review screen** — goal-by-goal evaluation. Per-goal card displays name, weightage, pillar, lifecycle state, full description, **measurement criteria**, achievement %, computed score, employee self-rating + self-comments, plus L1 rating panel. Action bar: Save draft · Sendback (discards L1 eval per FR-M09-016) · Submit to calibration. Submit blocked if any goal missing L1 rating + comments, overall comments < 50 chars, or plan weightage sum != 100.

- **FR-P03-003 Process Guidance Chat Agent** — extends P03 Chat Agent with step-by-step workflow guidance (apply leave, raise grievance, etc.) grounded on platform-managed help guides. Same accessibility envelope as FR-P03-001 (all active employee roles + pre-joining candidates).

- **FR-P03-004 Insurance ****&**** Benefits Chat Agent** — answers group insurance and benefits queries grounded on M11 INSURANCE_BENEFITS-categorised documents. Same governance ceilings as FR-P03-001. Phase 1 via M11 upload; deeper M14 integration Phase 3.

- **FR-M08-004 Offer letter generation — joint capability.** Both **Recruitment Admin AND HR Admin** can generate offer letters. Recruiter remains explicitly excluded. Role definitions for HR Admin and Recruitment Admin updated to reflect the joint capability; Recruiter definition clarifies the exclusion. Below-threshold offers can be generated directly by either role; above-threshold offers require CEO co-signature (unchanged).

- **§3.3 Entity scope clarification — no transactions cross entity boundaries.** Added an explicit paragraph at the head of §3.3 documenting that, in a Group Company deployment with multiple legal entities under a single tenant, every operational transaction (leave, attendance, payroll, statutory filings, FnF, grievance) stays within the employee’s entity. **Cross-entity HR visibility** (group headcount, group attrition, ex-employee history across entities, internal mobility scouting across entities) is owned by **Org Admin** per §3.2 — HR Admin remains single-entity scoped. **Platform Super Admin is cross-tenant** (all customers) and is NOT the channel for cross-entity visibility within a single tenant. Glossary Entity definition extended with the same rule.

- **FR-P03-005 Leadership AI Chat Agent — restricted to HR Admin and CEO.** Implements all 8 Leadership & PMO use cases from the AI Use Cases Prioritisation document (Section 5): Project Risk Notice-Period Engineers (L1·P0), Project Movement Tracker (L2·P1), Skill-Based Engineer Search (L3·P1), Bench/Availability Search (L4·P1), Internal Mobility Match (L5·P2), Natural-Language Analytics Query (L6·P2), Leadership Briefing Generator (L7·P2), Predictive Headcount Forecast (L8·P3). Phase 1.5+ build with additional governance: RBAC at retrieval layer, no LLM-generated numbers (figures from M16 only), sandboxed query execution for text-to-query, DPDPA & Legal review required for sensitive use cases (L5/L7/L8). Distinct UI entry point from standard Policy Chat Agent.

- LWF state & rate derived from employees.location.state_master join.

- Billing status changes trigger an audit-log entry; bench > 30 days flags the employee on the HR Admin dashboard.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Employee marked ESIC-applicable but gross > ₹21,000 → ESIC flag reverts to Not applicable with audit-log note; HR Admin alerted.

- Multi-state employee (work-from-anywhere) → LWF state defaults to the office location of record; HR Admin can override per declaration.

- Circular reporting (data integrity bug) → org chart renders the broken path with an exclamation badge; HR Admin notified.

- If an anonymous grievance contains accidentally self-identifying content, the system warns the submitter before final submission.

### Amendment — FS reconciliation (3 June 2026, tracked).

**FR-M01-020 / §3.7.1 — **Add Employee and the full employee-detail tier are HR Admin only. Performance Admin is removed from the M01 employee-master tier (see tracked edits in FR-M01-020 above); it accesses employee context via M09 surfaces and retains all §3.7.5 performance permissions.

**FR-M01-005 / FR-M01-014 — Manager organisation roster. **A My organisation roster is provided to L1–L5 managers and HOD / UAG Head with a Direct-only / Direct-plus-indirect (full downward subtree) toggle, backed by getReportingChain(direction=down, depth=full) and scoped per §3.6. Drill-in opens the read-only scoped detail tier (FR-M01-020). The HOD department roster gains the same reporting-chain toggle. Separated, Pre-joining and Absconded employees are excluded from the visible roster, consistent with FR-M01-005.

**FR-M01-019 / §4.4.1 — Sourced display fields and birthday consent. **Project allocated, Work type and Performance appraisal period are not columns on employees (§4.4.1); they render read-only on the Employment tab sourced from their owning systems — Project allocated from the Project / UAG assignment (Org Admin → Assignment Framework, projects §4.2.21); Work type from an Org-Admin-configured employment custom field (custom_field_values §4.2.15); Performance appraisal period from M09 Performance. A self-managed boolean share_birthday_with_team (default FALSE, employee-editable) is added to the employee record to govern birthday-widget sharing (month and day only) per §10.2.4.

### M01 — Functional Specification v1 (Reference Source: M01_Employee_Master_Requirements_1.docx)

**Spec status.** Authored per the module Requirements Specification (canonical Part A–N template). All locked decisions from the source spec’s Part L are reproduced verbatim below as the contractual rules. Configuration values and approval flows are summarised; full configuration registers remain in the source spec.

**Decision-code namespace.** DM-ENG / DM-PRO series. 27 decisions catalogued.

#### M01.FS1 — Locked decisions register

Decisions sourced from M01_Employee_Master_Requirements_*1*.docx Part L. Each row carries the decision code, scope, the locked rule, supporting note, and lock-status.

| **Code** | **Area** | **Decision (locked rule)** | **Status** |
| --- | --- | --- | --- |
| DM-ENG01 | Employee ID format | Format: {entity_short_code}{6-digit sequence}. Immutable post-assignment. Rehire issues new ID with previous_employee_id link. | OPEN |
| DM-ENG02 | Rehire ID policy | New ID always issued. Prior record retained read-only. previous_employee_id reference field on new record. | OPEN |
| DM-ENG03 | Continuous service on rehire | Service gap threshold (default 180 days) configurable at tenant. If gap ≤ threshold: continuous_service = true; gratuity carries forward (Phase 2). If > threshold: fresh slate. | OPEN |
| DM-ENG04 | Group date of joining | Set on first employment in the group; never changes thereafter — not on cross-entity transfer, not on rehire. | OPEN |
| DM-ENG05 | Effective-date scheduled-job cadence | Daily at 00:00 IST. Catch-up logic if job missed: replays all staged changes with effective_date ≤ today. | OPEN |
| DM-ENG06 | Sensitive field change approval flow | PAN / Aadhaar / Passport / Bank changes by employee → HR Admin approval. HR Admin changes direct save. Office Admin bank entry direct write (per DM-PRO02). | OPEN |
| DM-ENG07 | Soft delete retention | Indefinite during Phase 1. Phase 2 statutory retention review will set purge eligibility. | OPEN |
| DM-ENG08 | DPDPA erasure request handling | Non-statutory PII scheduled for erasure per configured retention. Statutory fields (PAN, PF/ESI/UAN, payroll history) retained — legal basis documented. | OPEN |
| DM-ENG09 | Bulk-import dry-run failure threshold | Wave 1 P06 gate: < 0.5% failure rate. Above threshold aborts the load; HR Admin reviews dry-run report and re-runs. | OPEN |
| DM-ENG10 | Org chart traversal performance | Up to 4 levels rendered eagerly (BRS NFR p95 < 1500ms). Beyond 4 levels: lazy-loaded on user expand. Materialised view used for high-frequency queries. | OPEN |
| DM-ENG11 | Grievance anonymity scope | Anonymous flag opt-in by employee. HR Admin sees category and count only when set; identity revealed only to POSH ICC for POSH-category grievances. | OPEN |
| DM-ENG12 | Nominee allocation rule | Sum of nominee_percentage per class = 100. Incomplete allocation blocks save. Max 10 dependents per employee (tenant-configurable). | OPEN |
| DM-ENG13 | Absconding flag retention | Flag persists until HR Admin manually clears or initiates separation. No auto-clear. Path back: HR Admin clears flag with reason (audit-logged) or initiates M03 separation. | OPEN |
| DM-ENG15 | Statutory nominee data capture phase | Gratuity, PF, and ESI nominee data captured in Phase 1 during M02 onboarding even though operational consumption (payout, enrolment, ECR challan) is Phase 2 via M07/M06. Platform stores nominee data from day one; statutory modules read it when activated. Mandatory at probation confirmation for gratuity; at PF eligibility for PF nominee. | OPEN |
| DM-PRO01 | BRS DB01 | Sensitive field edit approval | Consistent with Product Vision |
| DM-PRO02 | BRS DB02 | Office Admin bank entry | Consistent with RBAC §3.4. |
| DM-PRO03 | BRS DB03 | Office Admin identity access | Platform-level grant — not con |
| DM-PRO04 | BRS DB04 | Effective date versioning | Consistent with Product Vision |
| DM-PRO05 | BRS DB05 | No hard deletes | Consistent with Product Vision |
| DM-PRO06 | BRS DB06 | Pre-joining document migration | Consistent with Product Vision |
| DM-PRO07 | BRS DB07 | Benefits preference in onboarding | Consistent with Product Vision |
| DM-PRO09 | BRS DB09 | Date of birth year display | Privacy consideration — inferr |
| DM-PRO10 | BRS DB10 | Profile completeness mandatory fields | Platform safety floor for data |
| DM-PRO11 | BRS DB11 | HOD / UAG Head compensation visibility | Consistent with RBAC §6. |
| DM-PRO12 | BRS DB12 | Org chart drill-down scope | Platform-layer enforcement — n |

## 5.3 M02 — Onboarding

**FR-M02-001: Auto-initiation of onboarding 14 days before DOJ**

**Description: **Onboarding initiates automatically 14 days before Date of Joining for candidates in the Pending list. Default trigger basis is DOJ; configurable to Date Added to Pending List.

**User story: ***As an Onboarding Admin, I want onboarding to auto-initiate for upcoming joiners so that the candidate experience is consistent and timely without manual intervention.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Scheduled job runs daily and identifies candidates with proposed_date_of_joining ≤ today + 14 days for whom onboarding has not yet been initiated.

- Each identified candidate gets an onboarding_instances row with state = INITIATED and initiated_by_user_id = SYSTEM.

- Pre-joining portal access is activated; candidate is emailed a portal access link.

- Auto-initiation configurable per process: enable/disable, trigger basis (DOJ vs Date Added), days before.

**Business rules:**

- Auto-completion of onboarding is disabled by default — admin completion preserves explicit review step.

- Onboarding can be initiated manually by Onboarding Admin / HR Admin at any time before the auto-initiation date.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a candidate’s offer is withdrawn before auto-initiation fires, the candidate is removed from the Pending list and no initiation occurs.

- If proposed_date_of_joining is revised mid-process, auto-initiation does not retrigger — manual initiation by Onboarding Admin is required if the DOJ moves beyond 14 days again.

**FR-M02-002: Auto-save of onboarding form every 30 seconds**

**Description: **Candidate-submitted onboarding form is auto-saved at 30-second intervals. Reduces data loss on session timeout.

**User story: ***As a Candidate completing my onboarding form, I want my entries automatically saved every 30 seconds so that I do not lose data if my session times out or my browser crashes.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Auto-save fires every 30 seconds while the form is in focus and has unsaved changes.

- Auto-save creates onboarding_form_responses rows with field_status = DRAFT for each field that changed since the last save.

- On explicit submit, field_status transitions to SUBMITTED.

- Candidate sees a ‘Last saved at HH:MM:SS’ indicator that updates after each successful auto-save.

**Business rules:**

- Auto-save interval is configurable by Onboarding Admin (default 30 seconds per migrated config).

- Auto-save is enabled by default; can be disabled per process if required.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a network failure occurs during auto-save, the form state is held client-side and retried on reconnect; if the retry succeeds, the saved-at indicator updates retroactively.

- Auto-save does not bypass server-side field validation — validation errors are surfaced inline but do not block the auto-save itself; field_status remains DRAFT.

**FR-M02-003: Sign-Off cluster mandatory completion**

**Description: **Sign-Off Documents Cluster is mandatory at the process level (POSH, Code of Conduct, IT AUP, etc.). Candidate must complete sign-off on every document in this cluster before onboarding can be marked complete.

**User story: ***As an Onboarding Admin, I want to enforce mandatory sign-off on compliance-critical documents so that no employee starts work without acknowledging policies that carry legal or regulatory implications.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Sign-Off cluster is set as mandatory at process level. Cannot be disabled in Phase 1.

- Every document in the Sign-Off cluster generates a signoff_transactions row in PENDING state on onboarding initiation.

- Completion conditions check verifies: all sign-off transactions for the onboarding instance are in ACKNOWLEDGED state.

- Onboarding cannot be marked complete (state → COMPLETED) until this condition is met.

**Business rules:**

- Undertaking cluster is enabled but not mandatory — cluster contents may legitimately be empty for some employee populations (e.g. consultants).

- Reference cluster is read-only documents — no candidate action required; always enabled, never mandatory.

- Auto-rejection on DOJ change is a per-cluster toggle — if enabled and DOJ changes, all documents in that cluster reset to PENDING.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a candidate declines a sign-off document with allow_decline = TRUE, mandatory completion is not met and HR Admin is notified to resolve.

- If a sign-off document is updated to a new version during the candidate’s onboarding, the candidate must re-acknowledge the new version — the prior acknowledgement does not roll forward.

**FR-M02-004: BGV initiation and result handling**

**Description: **Background Verification is triggered on offer acceptance with the configured BGV package. Onboarding can complete with BGV in progress; HR Admin warned but can proceed. Discrepancy results require disposition by BGV reviewer.

**User story: ***As an Onboarding Admin with BGV review capability flag, I want to track BGV progress and act on Discrepancy results so that we can balance time-to-productivity against verification rigour.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- On offer acceptance, an outbound integration call to the configured BGV vendor creates a verification case.

- Inbound webhook from BGV vendor populates bgv_results rows per check with status (PENDING, VERIFIED, DISCREPANCY, UNABLE_TO_VERIFY).

- Verification Reports tab on candidate records visible only to users with BGV review capability flag.

- Discrepancy results require disposition: RETAIN, INVESTIGATE, or INITIATE_SEPARATION.

**Business rules:**

- BGV vendor ‘Allow BGV Admin to edit user inputs’ = No per migrated config — candidate input is treated as canonical record at submission.

- Onboarding can complete with BGV in progress — HR Admin sees a warning but can proceed.

- Post-completion, BGV results history is visible on Employee Profile to users with BGV review flag.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a Discrepancy result is dispositioned as INITIATE_SEPARATION post-onboarding, the M03 separation workflow is triggered with a system note identifying the BGV result reference.

- If the BGV vendor integration is unavailable for an extended period, manual entry of vendor-supplied results by BGV reviewer is permitted with the source URL captured for audit.

**FR-M02-005: Welcome page with Group Company display**

**Description: **Candidate-facing welcome page shows Group Company name primary with legal entity name secondary (smaller font) for transparency.

**User story: ***As a Candidate joining a Group Company, I want to see both the brand I am joining and the specific legal entity I am being employed by so that my employment context is clear.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Welcome page template renders {{group_company_name}} prominently and {{legal_entity_name}} in smaller secondary text.

- For Standalone deployments, only the legal_entity_name is shown (no Group Company).

- Welcome page is configurable per onboarding process by Onboarding Admin.

**Business rules:**

- Display configuration toggle ‘Display Group Company as Company Name on Welcome Page’ = Yes by default per migrated config.

- Welcome page can be customised with logo, brand colours, and a custom welcome message.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If group_company_name is not configured at the tenant level (Standalone deployment), the toggle is hidden in the configuration UI.

### M02 — Functional Specification v1 (Reference Source: M02_Onboarding_Requirements.docx)

**Spec status.** Authored per the module Requirements Specification (canonical Part A–N template). All locked decisions from the source spec’s Part L are reproduced verbatim below as the contractual rules. Configuration values and approval flows are summarised; full configuration registers remain in the source spec.

**Module boundary:** M02 owns the candidate-to-employee transition: from candidate-record initiation (handover from M08 Recruitment) through onboarding form completion, document collection, BGV, workflow tasks, and admin approval. On completion, approved data flows into the M01 Employee Master record and the lifecycle state advances from Pre-joining to Active (probation). Confirmation from probation is owned by M01 §C lifecycle and is OUT OF SCOPE for this document.

**Decision-code namespace.** DO series. 26 decisions catalogued.

#### M02.FS1 — Locked decisions register

Decisions sourced from M02_Onboarding_Requirements.docx Part L. Each row carries the decision code, scope, the locked rule, supporting note, and lock-status.

