## 5.6 M05 — Attendance

**FR-M05-001: Both-punch present-day rule with zero grace time**

**Description: **A day is considered present only when both a clock-in and a clock-out are recorded. Single punch (clock-in only or clock-out only) does not constitute a present day — the day is flagged as INCOMPLETE and treated as absent until regularised. Zero grace time for both clock-in and clock-out across all migrated policies.

**User story: ***As an Attendance Admin, I want strict both-punch enforcement and zero grace time so that attendance data integrity is preserved and Late By / Early Out are tracked accurately.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- attendance_records.status is set to PRESENT only when both clock_in_time and clock_out_time are populated.

- Single-punch days are marked as INCOMPLETE and treated as absent until regularised.

- Late By minutes is calculated immediately as any positive delta between clock_in_time and shift start_time.

- Early Out minutes is calculated immediately as any positive delta between shift end_time and clock_out_time.

**Business rules:**

- Zero grace time applies to ATPY_4, ATPY_5, ATPY_1 — all three migrated policies.

- Mark attendance based on = BOTH_IN_AND_OUT for ATPY_4 and ATPY_5. ATPY_1 (CEO) uses the same default.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If an employee clocks in but device fails before clock-out (e.g. biometric outage), the day is INCOMPLETE; employee must submit regularisation with reason and message.

- Mid-shift clock-out due to early departure (genuine half-day leave) is reconciled via half-day leave application overlapping the attendance record.

**FR-M05-002: Overnight punch and outside-fence approval**

**Description: **If an employee is marked absent on day D and clocks in on day D+1, the system prompts the employee to choose: (a) New day’s clock-in, or (b) Overnight last punch (continuation of day D’s shift). If in-fence: auto-update day D record. If outside-fence: route to L1 Manager for approval.

**User story: ***As an Employee on shift work, I want to record overnight last-punches without triggering an absence flag so that my shift attendance is accurately captured.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- On clock-in on day D+1 when day D was marked absent, the prompt is shown to the employee.

- If employee selects ‘Overnight last punch’ AND the punch is within the configured geofence (clock_in_in_geofence = TRUE), attendance_records for day D is updated immediately.

- If the punch is outside the geofence, an attendance_requests row with request_type = OVERNIGHT_OUTSIDE_FENCE is created and routed to L1 Manager.

- On L1 Manager approval, day D’s attendance_records is updated retroactively.

**Business rules:**

- If employee selects ‘New day’s clock-in’, the day D+1 record is created as a normal entry and day D remains absent.

- This functionality is enabled per migrated config — applies to all three policies.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If L1 Manager rejects the overnight outside-fence request, day D remains absent. Employee may submit a regularisation request as a fallback (subject to 32-day back-dating window and 7-per-month limit).

**FR-M05-003: Attendance request types with auto-approval disabled**

**Description: **Five attendance request types: regularisation, Out Duty, planned overtime, overnight outside-fence punch, shift change. Auto-approval is disabled for ALL types platform-wide — deliberate override of the migrated source defaults.

**User story: ***As an Attendance Admin, I want all attendance request types to require explicit approval so that data integrity is preserved against accidental auto-approval of incorrect punches.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- attendance_requests entity supports all five request types via request_type enum.

- Auto-approval setting on attendance policies is platform-locked at FALSE for all five types.

- The source Darwinbox auto-approve settings for check-in requests, attendance regularisation, out duty, and shift change must NOT be restored during migration configuration.

- Every request routes to L1 Manager at submission. HRBP, HR Admin, and Attendance Admin are eligible to approve from Stage 1 (no waiting period required for these roles).

**Business rules:**

- Escalation fires after 5 days of inaction (vs 10 days for leave).

- Payroll cutoff cadence: on 24th and 25th of each month, escalation cadence overrides to every 2 hours until all pending requests resolved. Applies to all attendance request types except shift change (admin-initiated).

- Shift change requests are admin-initiated and not subject to the 2-hourly cadence.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Rejection reason mandatory — system blocks rejection without reason.

- Concurrent leave + attendance restriction: Out Duty restricted on same day as leave. Half-day leave and short leave have no concurrent restriction.

**FR-M05-004: Out Duty present-day treatment**

**Description: **An approved Out Duty day is counted as a present day for all payroll and LOP purposes. Treated identically to a normal working day. The day appears labelled ‘Out Duty’ in the attendance register and reports for visibility but carries no payroll penalty.

**User story: ***As an Employee performing field work or client visits, I want approved Out Duty to count as present so that my pay is not impacted by legitimate work-related absences from the office.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- attendance_records.status = OUT_DUTY on approved Out Duty days.

- OUT_DUTY status counts as a present day for LOP calculation (no deduction).

- Full work duration recorded per shift — no LOP deduction, no leave balance impact.

- Day labelled ‘Out Duty’ in attendance register and reports for visibility.

**Business rules:**

- Unapproved or pending Out Duty request does not prevent the day from being marked absent — the absent status resolves only on approval.

- Out Duty back-dating window = 32 calendar days (ATPY_4/5) or 30 days (ATPY_1).

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If Out Duty is approved retroactively for a day already marked LOP and a payroll cycle has closed, the LOP correction must wait until the next payroll cycle (Phase 2).

**FR-M05-005: Absconding detection — 7 consecutive calendar days**

**Description: **Employee flagged as absconding after 7 consecutive calendar days of unauthorised absence. Weekly Off included in threshold. Only approved leave pauses the counter — pending leave does not pause. Configurable per tenant by Org Admin and per entity by HR Admin.

**User story: ***As an HR Admin, I want absconding detection on a 7-day calendar threshold so that unauthorised absences are surfaced quickly and consistently.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Scheduled job runs daily and identifies employees with 7 consecutive calendar days of attendance_records.status IN (ABSENT, INCOMPLETE).

- Weekly Off days are counted in the 7-day calendar threshold (matching source policy).

- Holidays are NOT counted in the threshold.

- System-deducted leave days are NOT counted; pending leave applications are NOT excluded from absconding consideration.

**Business rules:**

- Only approved leave pauses the counter.

- Threshold default is 7 calendar days; Org Admin override per tenant and HR Admin override per entity is permitted.

- On detection, employees.absconding_flagged_at is set and lifecycle_state transitions to ABSCONDED.

- Notification to HR Admin queue with employee details, last seen date, and counter calculation.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Absconding is not terminal — HR Admin can restore to ACTIVE if absence is justified or convert to separation via M03.

- Absconding flag survives until HR Admin explicit action — clock-in on day 8+ does not auto-clear the flag.

**FR-M05-006: Attendance lock and unlock with two modes**

**Description: **Attendance Admin and HR Admin can unlock a locked period. Two modes: (a) Full period unlock for the entity, (b) Single-record unlock for a specific employee and date range. No separate approval workflow — performed directly and logged immutably. Manager cannot unlock any period.

**User story: ***As an Attendance Admin, I want two unlock modes — full period and single record — so that I can handle corrections at the right granularity.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Attendance Admin or HR Admin can unlock periods. Manager cannot unlock under any configuration.

- Full period unlock reopens the entire attendance period for all employees in the entity.

- Single-record unlock reopens a specific employee’s record for a specific date range without affecting the rest of the period.

- Both unlock actions are performed directly without separate approval workflow but are logged immutably in P05 with admin identity, scope, and reason.

**Business rules:**

- Attendance lock fires at the payroll cutoff (25th of each month) via a scheduled job.

- Locked attendance_records have is_locked = TRUE and cannot be modified by employees or managers.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Unlocking a period after LOP feed has been sent to M06 (Phase 2) requires explicit Payroll Admin acknowledgement on the next payroll cycle.

**FR-M05-007: Regularisation monthly limit of 7**

**Description: **Maximum 7 attendance regularisation requests per calendar month per employee for ATPY_4 and ATPY_5. The 8th request in a month is blocked at submission. CEO policy (ATPY_1) has no limit.

**User story: ***As an Attendance Admin, I want a hard monthly regularisation limit so that the regularisation channel is not abused as a substitute for normal attendance.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Submission validation counts regularisation requests by employee within the calendar month.

- If count ≥ 7 and policy is ATPY_4 or ATPY_5, submission is blocked with a clear error message.

- ATPY_1 (CEO) has no monthly limit configured.

- Counter resets at midnight on the first day of each calendar month.

**Business rules:**

- Withdrawn or cancelled requests still count toward the monthly limit (the action of submission is what is being limited).

- No weekly, quarterly, or yearly cap applies — only the monthly limit.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If the employee hits the limit and has a legitimate need, HR Admin or Attendance Admin can raise the request on behalf as an exception, audit-logged.

**FR-M05-018: Clock-In / Clock-Out — direct attendance capture**

**Description: **Clock-In / Clock-Out is the time-tracking modality used to record the exact start and end of an employee’s workday. Available on **both web and mobile**. Captures hour/minute/second of CLOCK IN and CLOCK OUT. Clock-In requests do NOT route through manager approval — punches are recorded directly. IP-restriction is the primary verification mechanism (employees can only clock in from configured office network ranges); biometric mapping is the secondary mechanism when configured.

**User story: ***As an Employee working from the office, I want to click CLOCK IN at the start of my workday and CLOCK OUT at the end, so that my hours are recorded automatically without needing manager approval each time.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- CLOCK IN action records timestamp at second-precision when the employee initiates the action; CLOCK OUT records the closing timestamp and the platform computes total worked hours for the day.

- Clock-In is available on both web portal and mobile app. No selfie or GPS capture is taken in standard mode.

- IP-restriction blocks Clock-In from IP ranges outside the configured permitted list.

- Successful Clock-In / Clock-Out is recorded as a directly committed attendance event — does NOT generate a manager-approval request.

- Tracking Shift Hours: the live view shows scheduled shift hours alongside total time worked, helping employees stay aware of their position relative to the shift window.

**Business rules:**

- Clock-In modality is assigned per employee by HR Admin / Employee Admin (see FR-M05-020).

- If an employee misses a Clock-In or Clock-Out punch, they must use the attendance regularisation / adjustment flow (FR-M05-007) to correct the record. The CLOCK IN button cannot back-date a punch.

- Overnight punches (Clock-In on day N, Clock-Out on day N+1) follow FR-M05-002 outside-fence approval rule.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Network failure during Clock-In on mobile: timestamp queued locally and synced when network restores; if sync > 30 minutes after the original action, the synced punch is flagged for review.

- IP outside permitted range: action blocked with explicit message naming the violated rule and suggesting Check-In as alternative if assigned.

- Duplicate Clock-In (already clocked in for the day): action rejected with “You are already clocked in. Did you mean CLOCK OUT?”

**FR-M05-019: Check-In / Check-Out — location-verified attendance with GPS ****&**** selfie**

**Description: **Check-In / Check-Out is the location-verified attendance modality designed for the mobile workforce — field employees, remote/WFH employees, employees working out of client location, and any case where IP-based verification is not feasible. Captures GPS coordinates, geo-fence validation, and optionally a selfie. Available on **both web and mobile** (mobile is the primary platform; web capture is for exceptional cases such as missing mobile device). Check-In requests CAN require manager approval depending on policy configuration.

**User story: ***As a Field Sales Employee visiting a client site, I want to Check-In via my mobile so that my attendance is verified by GPS and a selfie, even though I’m not on the office network.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- CHECK IN action captures: timestamp, GPS coordinates (latitude/longitude), geo-fence match status (within / outside configured fence), optional selfie image, device identifier, network type (Wi-Fi / cellular).

- Geo-fence configuration per location: HR Admin / Office Admin defines a coordinate centre + radius (M01 location entity); Check-In within the fence is auto-marked Present; Check-In outside the fence routes to the configured approval flow.

- Selfie capture optional by default; HR Admin can mandate selfie capture per attendance policy.

- Check-In requests **can be configured to require manager (L1) approval** — even when within geo-fence — when the attendance policy check_in_requires_approval flag is enabled. Default OFF.

- Manager approval queue surfaces pending Check-In requests with the captured GPS, selfie thumbnail, and fence-match status visible to the approver.

**Business rules:**

- Check-In modality is assigned per employee by HR Admin / Employee Admin (see FR-M05-020).

- Approval flow: when approval is required, request routes to the employee’s L1 Manager. Manager can Approve, Reject, or Approve-with-comment. Rejected Check-Ins do not count toward present-day calculation.

- Selfie images and GPS coordinates are PII tier-2 — encrypted at rest, retention 90 days for routine attendance + 7 years for any record involved in a dispute, access-logged.

- Outside-fence Check-In: when approved, defaults the location tag to “Off-site” for reporting; when rejected, employee can re-submit with regularisation if there was a legitimate reason.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- GPS permission denied on device: Check-In blocked with explicit message; employee directed to enable location services or contact HR Admin for IP-based Clock-In re-assignment.

- Selfie capture failed (camera permission, hardware): if selfie is mandatory, action blocked; if optional, Check-In proceeds without selfie with the audit-log noting “selfie unavailable”.

- Geo-fence drift due to GPS inaccuracy: GPS uncertainty radius > 100 m triggers a soft warning and audit-log flag, but does not auto-reject.

**FR-M05-020: Attendance modality assignment per employee**

**Description: **HR Admin / Employee Admin assigns the applicable attendance capture modality (Clock-In, Check-In, or both) per employee at the time of adding the employee to the system, with the ability to change the assignment later for role transfers (e.g. office-based employee moves to field role). Both modalities are available on both web and mobile — the per-employee assignment determines which buttons are visible to the employee.

**User story: ***As an HR Admin onboarding a new field sales employee, I want to assign Check-In/Out as their attendance modality (instead of Clock-In/Out) so that their mobile workflow captures GPS and selfie verification by default.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Add Employee form (M01) includes an Attendance modality picker with options: Clock-In only · Check-In only · Both (employee selects per action).

- Modality assignment defaults from the employee’s location attendance policy (HR Admin configures the default per location); explicit per-employee override always wins.

- When modality changes (e.g. role transfer), historical attendance records retain their original modality tag; new punches follow the new modality.

- Employee Profile › Employment tab displays the current modality (read-only to employee, editable by HR Admin / Employee Admin).

- Bulk reassignment by HR Admin / Employee Admin supported via the employee data import workflow; each row contains the modality column.

**Business rules:**

- Both modalities available on both platforms (web + mobile). The per-employee assignment governs which buttons the employee sees on their attendance page — never the platform.

- Modality changes are audit-logged: who changed, when, from-value, to-value, reason text (optional).

- An employee with “Both” modality assignment sees both Clock-In and Check-In buttons; the system records which modality was used for each punch in the attendance_log entity.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Employee without a modality assignment (data integrity gap, e.g. migrated legacy record) → defaults to Clock-In with a HR Admin dashboard flag for follow-up.

- Modality removed mid-day (e.g. employee transitioned from Clock-In to Check-In at 14:00): the morning Clock-In is preserved; the day’s Clock-Out can be done via either modality with audit-log noting the transition.

### FR-M05-008 — Planned Overtime

**Description.** Allow employees and managers to pre-plan overtime windows ahead of the actual work, with approval flow distinct from regularisation of completed OT.

**Trigger.** Planned OT request submission.

**Inputs.** Date, planned start/end time, OT policy applicable to employee, planned reason, enable_planned_overtime setting.

**Processing.**

- Validate request against OT policy caps (daily/weekly/monthly).

- Route through configured approval flow (may differ from completed-OT regularisation flow).

- On approval, mark window as pre-approved.

- When actual OT is recorded, auto-link to pre-approved window if hours match within tolerance.

- If hours exceed pre-approved window, route excess through standard OT approval.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- Pre-approved OT windows visible on employee schedule.

- Auto-matching tolerance configurable per policy.

- Excess hours auto-routed without manual re-submission.

### FR-M05-009 — Infraction Framework

**Description.** Track attendance infractions (late, early out, missed punch, absent without leave, absconding precursor) and accumulate points / counts against employees. Triggers warning workflows at configured thresholds.

**Trigger.** Daily attendance close cron.

**Inputs.** Attendance records, infraction policy thresholds (enable_infraction_framework, use_points_system_in_infraction), absconding configuration.

**Processing.**

- Classify each attendance day into one of: PRESENT, LATE, EARLY_OUT, MISSED_PUNCH, ABSENT, ABSENT_ON_LEAVE.

- Assign infraction points per policy.

- Accumulate points over rolling window.

- When threshold crossed, trigger configured action (notify, warning letter, suspend self-service, escalate to manager).

- Absconding precursor: when employee absent without leave for N consecutive calendar days, flag and route to HRBP per consider_weekly_off_for_absconding / consider_holiday_for_absconding.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- Points configurable per infraction type.

- Rolling window configurable (30, 60, 90, 180 days).

- Thresholds trigger configured actions.

- Absconding flag respects platform-level inclusion settings.

### FR-M05-010 — Night Shift Differential (NSD)

**Description.** Compute and pay a differential for hours worked during night-shift bands (typically 22:00 – 06:00 local).

**Trigger.** Attendance close, payroll cycle close.

**Inputs.** Attendance records with punch times, shift definition with NSD multiplier, NSD time band, applicable day class.

**Processing.**

- For each attendance record, compute hours falling within NSD band.

- Apply NSD multiplier (nsd_multiplier_value) to qualifying hours.

- Cascade multipliers if day-class additional multiplier applies (e.g. holiday + NSD).

- Pass adjusted hours and pay computation to payroll.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- NSD applied per band intersection, not whole shift.

- Multiple multipliers (NSD + holiday + weekly-off) stack per policy.

- Audit log captures stack composition.

### FR-M05-012 — Attendance Register

**Description.** Tenant-configurable comprehensive attendance register showing per-employee per-day status with shift, punches, requests, system deductions, and weekly-off / holiday markers.

**Trigger.** Attendance Register UI access, report export.

**Inputs.** Attendance records, requests, leave deductions, shift assignments, holiday calendar, platform-level inclusion toggles.

**Processing.**

- Compose per-cell content based on configured toggles (show_system_deduction_in_register, show_attendance_requests_in_register, show_shift_details_in_register, show_weekly_off_in_register).

- Apply colour-coding to status types.

- Export to PDF/XLSX with same composition.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- Register loads under 5 seconds for 200-employee × 31-day grid.

- Colour-coding accessible (WCAG AA contrast).

- Export preserves all visible cells.

### FR-M05-013 — Mobile Widget Configuration

**Description.** Tenant-controlled visibility of 17 mobile attendance widgets, per cluster 24 in attendance_platform_settings.

**Trigger.** Mobile app load.

**Inputs.** Platform-level hide toggles for each widget.

**Processing.**

- Resolve hide toggles for the tenant.

- Filter widget list at mobile app load.

- Re-render layout when toggles change.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- Each widget independently toggleable.

- Layout reflows correctly when widgets hidden.

- Push-refresh on toggle change reaches mobile within 5 minutes.

### FR-M05-014 — LOP Report Generation

**Description.** Periodic LOP report listing all loss-of-pay days per employee for payroll processing.

**Trigger.** Scheduled cron per lop_report_send_date.

**Inputs.** LOP report configuration cluster (include_absent_in_lop_report, include_sa_in_lop_report, lop_report_months, lop_report_cycle_start_date, lop_report_send_to_user_ids).

**Processing.**

- Compute LOP days per employee for configured cycle.

- Include or exclude Absent and Single Attendance per toggles.

- Generate XLSX with per-employee summary and per-day detail.

- Email to configured recipients.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- Report generated on cron date without manual intervention.

- Recipients receive attachment within 30 minutes of cron.

- Report exportable on-demand outside cron.

### FR-M05-015 — Continuous Working Days Tracking

**Description.** Monitor employees working without rest day breaks and alert at configured thresholds.

**Trigger.** Daily attendance close.

**Inputs.** Attendance records, continuous_working_days_notify_threshold, exclude_days_in_continuous_count, continuous_notify_receivers.

**Processing.**

- For each employee, compute longest current consecutive working streak excluding configured day classes.

- If streak ≥ threshold, generate alert.

- Notify configured receivers.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- Streak resets on any excluded-class day or absence.

- Notifications fire once per threshold crossing.

- Visible on employee profile and manager dashboard.

### FR-M05-016 — Break Policy Configuration

**Description.** Activate break-policy framework controlling break durations, frequency, and pay treatment per shift.

**Trigger.** Break punch in/out, attendance close.

**Inputs.** Break policy configuration on shift, break punches, enable_break_policy_config.

**Processing.**

- Validate break in/out punches against min_break_duration_minutes, max_break_duration_minutes, max_breaks_per_shift.

- Auto-deduct break time per auto_deduct_break_if_not_punched.

- Compute net work hours per is_break_paid.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- Break punches honour min/max validation.

- Auto-deduction transparent in attendance display.

- Paid vs unpaid break configurable per policy.

### FR-M05-017 — Overtime Indexing

**Description.** Apply non-linear pay multipliers across OT hour bands (e.g. first 10 hours at 1.5×, next 10 at 1.75×, beyond at 2.0×) using overtime_indexing_rules.

**Trigger.** OT computation at cycle close.

**Inputs.** Attendance OT minutes, applicable indexing rule, day class, slabs array.

**Processing.**

- Sort slabs by from_hours.

- Walk OT hours; for each slab, apply slab multiplier to hours falling within slab range.

- Sum products for total weighted OT pay.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- Slabs evaluated continuously without gaps.

- Audit log records per-slab breakdown.

- Indexing rule changes apply prospectively.

### M05 — Functional Specification v1 (Reference Source: M05_Attendance_Requirements.docx)

**Spec status.** Authored per the module Requirements Specification (canonical Part A–N template). All locked decisions from the source spec’s Part L are reproduced verbatim below as the contractual rules. Configuration values and approval flows are summarised; full configuration registers remain in the source spec.

**Decision-code namespace.** DA series. 22 decisions catalogued.

