## 5.8 M09 — Performance Management

**FR-M09-001: Appraisal-period-bound OKR goal plan**

**Description: **OKR goal methodology with **one active goal plan per employee at a time**, scoped to the employee’s current appraisal period. The appraisal period is defined per employee by Performance Admin or HR Admin (joint authority) — initial period defaults to start on the employee’s date of joining; subsequent periods default to start on the day after the previous period ends. Default period duration is 12 months but is editable per employee at plan creation (supports off-cycle reviews, accelerated cycles for fast-track promotions, and prorated first-period cycles for mid-year joiners). The platform operates 12 monthly goal cycles and 12 monthly review cycles at the tenant level (JAN through DEC cohorts); each employee is slotted into one cohort. The next goal plan transitions from PENDING to DRAFT one month before the current plan’s plan_end_date so the employee has time to author the next cycle’s goals before the current cycle closes.

**User story: ***As a Performance Admin (or HR Admin), I want to define each employee’s appraisal period explicitly so that goals are bound to a single, well-defined performance window — accommodating mid-year joiners, off-cycle reviews, and accelerated promotion tracks — without the ambiguity of multiple overlapping plans.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Each employee has **at most one ACTIVE goal_plans row at any time**. State enum: PENDING · DRAFT · ACTIVE · IN_REVIEW · CLOSED · CANCELLED. State transitions: PENDING (auto-created on previous plan ACTIVE) → DRAFT (30 days before plan_start_date — employee can edit) → ACTIVE (on plan_start_date) → IN_REVIEW (on plan_end_date, or earlier when an admin opens the review on demand per FR-M09-030) → CLOSED (after manager and HR review complete). Multiple rows may coexist per employee but only one in ACTIVE state; a PENDING/DRAFT row for the next cycle can overlap with an ACTIVE row for the current cycle (this is the 1-month overlap window). CLOSED and CANCELLED rows are historical.

- Appraisal period (plan_start_date, plan_end_date) is set by Performance Admin or HR Admin at plan creation in the M09 admin surface (joint authority). The system pre-fills the dates per the default rules below; either role can edit either or both dates before activation.

- **Cohort derivation (15th-of-month cutoff)**: the monthly_cohort is derived from the anchor date — if the anchor day ≤ 15 the cohort is the anchor month, if > 15 it is the next month. Anchor = date_of_joining (first plan) or previous plan_end_date + 1 day (subsequent plans).

- **First plan default dates**: plan_start_date = the **1st of the derived cohort month** · plan_end_date = plan_start_date + 12 months − 1 day. Example: DOJ 14 Mar → MAR cohort → 1 Mar – 28/29 Feb; DOJ 22 Mar → APR cohort → 1 Apr – 31 Mar.

- **Subsequent plan default dates**: anchor = previous plan_end_date + 1 day; cohort re-derived via the 15th cutoff; plan_start_date = 1st of that cohort month · plan_end_date = plan_start_date + 12 months − 1 day. (In steady state the cohort is stable year-on-year.)

- **Auto-open of next plan (1 month early)**: the next goal plan transitions from PENDING to DRAFT state **30 days before** the current plan’s plan_end_date. This gives the employee a one-month window to author goals for the upcoming cycle before the current cycle transitions to IN_REVIEW on plan_end_date. The cron job creates the next plan row in PENDING state at the moment the current plan transitions to ACTIVE; on (plan_end_date − 30 days), the cron promotes PENDING → DRAFT and notifies the employee, L1 Manager, and Performance Admin / HR Admin. Performance Admin or HR Admin reviews period dates and goal-cohort assignment in the M09 admin surface before the plan moves from DRAFT to ACTIVE on plan_start_date.

- plan_end_date is editable per employee. Both shorter (e.g. 6 months for an accelerated cycle) and longer (e.g. 15 months for a delayed cycle alignment) periods are permitted. Minimum allowed period: 3 months. Maximum: 18 months. Outside these bounds requires HR Admin override with audit.

- Goal methodology is OKR — platform-fixed for Phase 1.

- Sub-goals (Key Results) supported via parent_goal_id self-reference.

- Each edit to plan_start_date or plan_end_date is audit-logged (period_set_by_user_id, period_set_at, source_role) and notifies the employee, L1 Manager, HRBP, and the other-role admin (e.g. if Performance Admin edits, HR Admin is notified; and vice-versa).

**Business rules:**

- Minimum goals required per plan: 2 (configured per migrated value).

- Maximum goals per plan: no upper limit configured.

- Maximum sub-goals per goal: 6 (configured per migrated value).

- Cycle pattern is platform — not configurable per tenant.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If an employee transitions to Notice state mid-plan, the active plan is paused; M03 separation flow handles closure.

**FR-M09-028: Goal plan assignment — cron default with admin approval, override, and manual assignment**

**Description: **Goal plans reach employees through a **cron-driven default** that Performance Admin or HR Admin (joint authority) can **approve as-is or override**, plus an explicit **manual assignment** path for cases the cron does not cover (new hires before the first cron run, off-cycle plans, accelerated promotion tracks, PIP-linked plans, and cohort re-slotting). Both the automated and manual paths converge on the same goal_plans row and the same DRAFT → ACTIVE gate.

**User story: ***As a Performance Admin (or HR Admin), I want the system to propose each employee’s next goal plan automatically, but retain the ability to approve, override, or manually assign plans, so routine cycles run hands-off while new hires, off-cycle reviews, and corrections can be handled on demand.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- **Cron default (unchanged from FR-M09-001).** The cron creates the next plan in PENDING when the current plan goes ACTIVE, and promotes PENDING → DRAFT 30 days before plan_start_date with default dates and default monthly_cohort (= calendar month of plan_start_date). This is the zero-touch path.

- **Approve.** In the M09 admin surface, Performance Admin or HR Admin can approve a cron-created DRAFT plan as-is; on plan_start_date it transitions to ACTIVE. No edit is required for the routine case.

- **Override.** Before activation, either admin can override the cron defaults on a DRAFT (or PENDING) plan: plan_start_date, plan_end_date, monthly_cohort, minimum_goals_required, and maximum_goals_allowed. Overrides are audit-logged (period_set_by_user_id, period_set_at, source_role) and notify the employee, L1 Manager, HRBP, and the other-role admin.

- **Manual single-employee assignment.** Either admin can manually create a goal plan for one employee on demand without waiting for the cron — selecting the employee, period (pre-filled per the default rules but editable), monthly_cohort, goal-count limits, and an assignment reason (NEW_HIRE · OFF_CYCLE · ACCELERATED · PIP · CORRECTION · OTHER). The plan is created directly in DRAFT (employee can author immediately) or PENDING (scheduled), at the admin’s choice. Blocked if the employee already has a PENDING/DRAFT/ACTIVE plan covering an overlapping window unless the admin confirms supersession of the existing plan (which moves the superseded row to CANCELLED with audit).

- **Manual bulk assignment.** Either admin can select multiple employees (by filter — entity, department, designation, joining-month, or explicit multi-select) and assign or move them in one action: create plans for a set of new hires, or re-slot a set into a different monthly_cohort. Bulk actions show a pre-commit summary (N employees, target cohort/period, conflicts flagged) and require explicit confirmation. Each resulting row is individually audit-logged.

- **Cohort re-slotting.** Moving an employee (or set) to a different monthly_cohort recomputes draft_open_date and the review-cycle binding; it does not change already-authored goals. Re-slotting an ACTIVE plan requires HR Admin override with audit.

- All four paths (cron, approve, override, manual) produce the same goal_plans schema and respect the one-ACTIVE-plan-per-employee rule.

**Business rules:**

- Manual assignment is restricted to Performance Admin and HR Admin (joint authority). No other role — including module admins outside M09 — can create or assign goal plans.

- A manual plan still passes through the DRAFT → ACTIVE gate; manual creation does not bypass goal authoring, L1 approval, or weightage validation.

- assignment_source on the plan records how it was created: CRON · MANUAL_SINGLE · MANUAL_BULK · OVERRIDE.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Manual assignment for an employee in PRE_JOINING state is allowed (plan created in PENDING, opens on joining); for SEPARATED/ABSCONDED state it is blocked.

- Bulk assignment partial failure: rows that violate the overlap rule are skipped and reported in the summary; valid rows still commit. No all-or-nothing rollback.

- Two admins assigning to the same employee concurrently: last-write-wins on the plan row; the second admin sees an overlap-conflict prompt.

**FR-M09-002: Goal field configuration — manual achievement entry**

**Description: **Goal fields: Goal Name, Goal Description, Goal Measurement (free-text criteria), Goal Achievement %, Goal Weightage, Goal Scorecard Pillar. These are the only goal-level fields the platform supports — there are no goal timeline, goal status, structured goal target, goal metric, custom-formula goal score, goal achievement matrix, or goal visibility fields.

**User story: ***As an HR Admin, I want simplified goal field set with manual achievement entry so that the goal-setting experience remains focused and avoids over-engineering.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Goal entity supports the five enabled fields with mandatory and approval rules per migrated config.

- Goal Achievement % is entered manually — auto-calculate is disabled per migrated config.

- Disabled fields are not exposed in the UI or API.

- Goal Plan Approver is the L1 Manager (Direct Manager) per migrated config.

**Business rules:**

- Goal Weightage limits (min/max per goal) not enforced per migrated config.

- The default goal-score formula (achievement_% × weightage, capped at 120%) applies to every goal; there is no per-goal custom score formula or score validation/cap field.

- Employees can add goals = Yes per migrated config.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a manager rejects a proposed goal, the goal returns to DRAFT state for the employee to revise; no balance impact since this is goal-setting, not consumption.

**FR-M09-003: Review stage workflow — Self → L1 Evaluator → Calibration → Acknowledgement**

**Description: **Four-stage review workflow: (1) Self review by employee, (2) L1 Evaluator review by direct manager, (3) Calibration moderation by HR Admin/Performance Admin/Skip-level, (4) Acknowledgement by employee.

**User story: ***As an L1 Manager, I want a structured four-stage review workflow so that performance evaluations are fair, calibrated, and acknowledged by the employee.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Each employee has one review_records row per cycle.

- Self review: employee fills self-review responses; submitted_at captured.

- L1 Evaluator review: L1 Manager fills l1_review_responses and draft_rating; submitted_at captured.

- Calibration: HR Admin or Performance Admin reviews draft_rating across calibration_records; final calibration_rating set.

- Acknowledgement: employee acknowledges or declines the published_rating.

**Business rules:**

- Draft ratings are restricted to Reviewer only — no entitlement grant can expose draft ratings prior to publish event.

- Skip-level Manager has read access to full reporting subtree calibration and retains access to published ratings after calibration window closes.

- HOD calibration access requires module-level entitlement grant by Org Admin.

- Dotted Line Manager has no appraisal access — confirmed by DM-PF01 lock.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If an employee transitions to Notice or Separated mid-cycle, the cycle is closed with the last completed stage’s data; manager is notified.

- Acknowledgement can be DECLINED — the employee provides a decline reason; the rating remains published but the disagreement is captured.

**FR-M09-004: Multi-Source Feedback by L1 Manager invitation**

**Description: **L1 Manager-initiated Multi-Source Feedback is the contributor mechanism. Contributors are runtime assignments only — no RBAC role or capability flag.

**User story:** ****As an L1 Manager, I want to invite peer feedback contributors during my team***’****s reviews so that the evaluation incorporates broader perspective than my own direct observation.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- L1 Manager invites contributors via the multi_source_feedback table — contributor_user_id is referenced and an invitation notification is sent.

- Contributor sees an in-app and email task to submit feedback.

- Feedback responses captured in multi_source_feedback.feedback_responses (JSON).

- State workflow: INVITED → SUBMITTED or DECLINED.

**Business rules:**

- Contributor identity may be visible to L1 Manager and HR Admin only — anonymisation is per cycle configuration.

- Contributors do not require any RBAC role grant or capability flag — they are runtime invitations.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a contributor does not respond within the cycle window, their invitation expires and is marked DECLINED by default.

**FR-M09-005: Probation confirmation — calendar-driven trigger**

**Description: **Probation confirmation is calendar-driven — fires at probation_end_date on the employment_history record. Cannot be manually bypassed without HR Admin override. L1 Manager recommendation, HR Admin confirmation.

**User story: ***As an HR Admin, I want probation confirmation to fire automatically at the configured date so that no employee inadvertently remains on probation beyond their due date.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Scheduled job runs daily and identifies employment_history rows with probation_flag = TRUE and probation_end_date ≤ today + 30 days.

- Notification fires to L1 Manager: ‘Probation confirmation due for {employee}. Please submit your recommendation.’

- L1 Manager submits recommendation: CONFIRM, EXTEND, or NOT_CONFIRM (terminate).

- HR Admin reviews and confirms; on confirmation, probation_flag is set FALSE and the appropriate confirmation letter (M11 template) is generated.

**Business rules:**

- Probation extension creates a new employment_history version with the updated probation_end_date.

- Not-confirm initiates the separation flow (M03) with category = INVOLUNTARY.

- Manual bypass requires HR Admin override and is audit-logged.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If L1 Manager does not submit recommendation by probation_end_date, escalation fires to next-level manager and HR Admin simultaneously.

- If the employee is on extended leave during probation_end_date, the confirmation can be deferred — captured as a flag on the record.

**FR-M09-015: Goal creation — flexible authorship with L1 edit-and-approve**

**Description: **Either the Employee or the L1 Manager can create a goal on the employee’s plan. Previous-cycle goals can be copied and modified to create new goals (the copy is a fresh goals row with its own ID, no link back to the source). L1 Manager can Approve, Reject, Sendback, or Edit-and-Approve. L1 edits are audit-logged and the employee is notified in-app and via email; employee can raise disagreement which is resolved through direct discussion with the L1 Manager (no separate workflow stage).

Goals are authored and finalised during the goal-planning stage (plan creation through L1 approval). Achievement and ratings are captured later, during the appraisal review period when the plan enters its monthly review cycle — there is no separate mid-cycle progress-tracking step.

**User story: ***As an L1 Manager, I want to be able to draft a goal directly for my reportee when needed, copy from a prior cycle’s goal, and refine wording during approval without bouncing the goal back, so that goal-setting moves fast.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Goal creation entry points: Employee from their plan page (default), L1 Manager from team page or reportee’s plan view, and “Copy from previous goal” picker accessible to both.

- **Copy from previous goal — scope:**

- **Employee: picker lists the employee’s OWN prior goals from earlier closed cycles. Max 50 most recent across all prior plans.**

- **L1 Manager:** picker lists prior goals rows from ANY current team member (direct reports per employees.manager_id = L1_user_id). Filter by reportee name + scorecard pillar + cycle. Goals from former reportees (manager_id changed) are excluded. Max 50 results per query; pagination beyond.

- Copy creates a fresh goals row with new goal_id, no parent_goal_id link to source. Pre-populated editable fields: goal_name, description, **goal_measurement**, scorecard_pillar, weightage, target. Source goal_id audit-logged in goals_history.copied_from_goal_id for traceability.

- Copy-and-modify creates a fresh goal record with new goal_id, no parent_goal_id link to source, and pre-populates editable fields (goal_name, description, scorecard_pillar, weightage, target).

- L1 Manager approval actions: Approve · Reject · Sendback · Edit-and-Approve. Edit-and-Approve commits L1’s changes and moves approval_state to APPROVED in one action.

- Edit-and-Approve audit log captures: editor user_id, edit timestamp, field-by-field old → new values, edit reason (optional free text).

- Employee notified within 5 minutes (in-app + email) when L1 uses Edit-and-Approve; notification names changed fields and shows old/new values.

**Business rules:**

- L1 Manager edits are silent on employee’s draft (no concurrency conflict): if employee is editing the same goal when L1 acts, L1’s action wins and employee’s draft changes are preserved as an audit-log entry but not applied.

- Goals created by L1 Manager skip the PENDING_APPROVAL state and land directly in APPROVED — manager is the approver, so self-approval is implicit. Audit-log marks created_and_approved_by_same_user = TRUE.

- Performance Admin bulk goal assignment is out of scope for Phase 1.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Copy-from-previous picker shows max 50 most recent goals across all prior plans; employee can filter by scorecard pillar or text search.

- If employee disagrees with L1’s edit, they cannot revert directly. Resolution path: direct discussion with the L1 Manager; if unresolved, raise grievance via FR-M01-009 (Manager category).

**FR-M09-016: Sendback discards L1’s prior evaluation**

**Description: **When L1 Manager sends a goal or review record back to the employee for revision, any L1 ratings, comments, or evaluations from that cycle are discarded. Employee resubmits; L1 evaluates fresh on the resubmitted version. Prevents stale evaluations from carrying forward inadvertently.

**User story: ***As an L1 Manager, I want sendback to clear my prior evaluation so that when the employee resubmits an updated goal, I start fresh and don’t accidentally publish stale ratings from before the change.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Sendback action on review_records moves the record back to Self stage and clears: l1_rating, l1_comments, l1_evaluated_at, l1_evaluator_user_id.

- Discarded L1 evaluation is preserved in review_records_history audit table with discarded_via = SENDBACK flag — not visible to employee, available to HR Admin for audit.

- Employee notified that sendback occurred with L1’s sendback reason (mandatory text).

- On resubmission, L1 sees a fresh evaluation form; the discarded prior evaluation is not pre-filled.

**Business rules:**

- Resolves locked decision DM-PF07 (sendback semantics).

- Sendback count tracked per cycle; if send_back_count >= max_send_back_iterations (from review_templates), further sendback is blocked and L1 must choose Approve or Reject.

- Sendback reason is mandatory and is exposed to the employee.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If sendback is triggered after MSF contributors have submitted feedback, MSF feedback is retained (only L1’s own evaluation is cleared).

- Sendback from Calibration stage (HR Admin / Performance Admin sends back to L1 for re-evaluation) is a separate path covered in FR-M09-022; the L1 evaluation under calibration sendback is NOT discarded (only Self-stage sendback discards).

**FR-M09-029: Admin-enabled goal editing — anytime, any employee**

**Description: Performance Admin and HR Admin (joint authority) can open goal editing for any employee at any time, independent of plan state, approval state, or role change. This is a standalone administrative capability — it is not triggered by, conditioned on, or limited to a role change or a transfer.**

**User story: ***As a Performance Admin, I want to unlock goal editing for any employee on demand — to fix an error, accommodate a re-scoped objective, or correct a mis-assigned goal — without waiting for a revision request or a role change.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Performance Admin or HR Admin can toggle “Enable goal editing” for a single employee, a selected set, or an entire goal plan, from the M09 admin surface, at any time.

- Enabling editing is not gated by approval_state or plan state (DRAFT/ACTIVE/IN_REVIEW), role change, or any edit limit. There is no maximum number of admin-enabled edits and no end-of-cycle cut-off.

- Once enabled, the employee (and L1 Manager) can edit the specified goals directly; the admin can also edit on the employee’s behalf.

- Every admin enablement event and each resulting edit is audit-logged (actor, employee, goals affected, timestamp, reason).

- Admin can scope the editing window (open-ended, or auto-close on a chosen date) and can re-lock editing at any time.

**Business rules:**

- This capability is restricted to Performance Admin and HR Admin (joint authority). No other role can enable goal editing outside the normal Employee/L1 goal-editing flow during goal planning.

- Goals retain their goal_achievement_pct unless explicitly changed during the edit.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Editing enabled during the IN_REVIEW stage: edits are permitted but flagged on the L1 appraisal screen so the reviewer sees the goal changed after review opened.

- Two admins toggling the same employee concurrently: last-write-wins on the editing-window state; both actions audit-logged.

**FR-M09-030: Admin on-demand review activation, pause, and resume**

**Description: **Performance Admin and HR Admin (joint authority) can manually open the appraisal review for an employee, a selected set, a filter-based cohort, or an entire monthly review cohort — at any time after goals are approved, independent of plan_end_date — and can pause and later resume an in-flight review. This complements the automatic period-end start (FR-M09-001) and the separation-triggered start (FR-M09-024); it does not replace them. Pausing freezes stage progression and SLA clocks and blocks employee/manager action without discarding entered data.

**User story: **As a Performance Admin, I want to open a reportee’s (or a cohort’s) review before the period formally ends — and to pause a review that’s mid-flight — so I can run off-cycle or accelerated appraisals and hold a review when circumstances require, without rewriting period dates or losing work already entered.

**Acceptance criteria:**

- **New goal_plans.review_initiation_source enum:** CALENDAR (auto on plan_end_date) · ADMIN_MANUAL (this FR) · SEPARATION (FR-M09-024). Captured on every ACTIVE — IN_REVIEW transition.

- **Open now:** from the M09 admin surface, either admin can move a plan ACTIVE → IN_REVIEW on demand for a single employee, multi-select, filter (entity/department/designation/cohort), or a whole monthly cohort. Opening early supersedes the pending calendar auto-transition for that plan (auto-start becomes a no-op).

- Opening requires the plan to be ACTIVE with all goals in APPROVED state and sum(goal_weightage) == 100; plans in PENDING/DRAFT/CLOSED/CANCELLED are rejected with an explicit reason.

- **Pause:** either admin can set a review on hold at any stage. New review_records fields — review_hold (boolean), hold_reason (mandatory), held_by_user_id, held_at — freeze the workflow: stage actions are blocked for employee, L1, and calibrators; SLA clocks (FR-M09-021) stop; no escalation fires while held. Entered self/L1 data is preserved.

- **Resume:** either admin clears the hold (resumed_by_user_id, resumed_at); the workflow re-opens at the exact stage it was paused in and SLA clocks restart from their remaining balance.

- Every open, pause, and resume action is audit-logged (actor, scope, affected employees, timestamp, reason).

- **Notifications:** open → employee + L1 (“Your review is now open”); pause → employee + L1 (“Review temporarily on hold”); resume → employee + L1; cohort actions → a summary to the initiating admin.

**Business rules:**

- Restricted to Performance Admin and HR Admin (joint authority). L1 Managers and all other roles are explicitly excluded — they cannot open, pause, or resume a review.

- An admin-opened review cannot be reverted to planning — there is no recall. Once IN_REVIEW, the review proceeds through its stages or is paused/resumed; correcting goals after opening uses Enable goal editing (FR-M09-029), and discarding an L1 evaluation uses sendback (FR-M09-016).

- The one-ACTIVE-plan and one-review-record-per-cycle rules (FR-M09-001, -003) still hold; manual open does not create a second review record.

- A paused review does not extend plan_end_date; the period clock and achievement entry are unaffected — only workflow progression and SLA are frozen.

- Manual open does not bypass any downstream gate: Self → L1 → Calibration → Publish, sendback (FR-M09-016), weightage validation, and publish irreversibility (FR-M09-024) all apply unchanged.

- Cohort-level open/pause/resume is all-or-nothing per the resolved scope, except rows failing a guardrail (e.g. non-APPROVED goals), which are skipped and reported — no full rollback.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Plan reaches plan_end_date while paused: it stays paused (calendar auto-start is suppressed); audit notes the deferred transition.

- Employee separates while a manual review is paused: the FR-M09-024 separation path takes precedence — HR Admin resumes/initiates with partial_cycle_flag = TRUE.

- Enable goal editing (FR-M09-029) opened while a review is paused: edits are permitted and flagged on the L1 screen on resume, consistent with FR-M09-029’s IN_REVIEW behaviour.

- Two admins acting on the same review concurrently: last-write-wins on the hold/state row; both actions audit-logged.

- Resume attempted on a CLOSED cycle: blocked — re-opening a closed cycle remains the HR Admin override path in FR-M09-024.

**FR-M09-PIP: Performance Improvement Plan (PIP)**

**Description: **HR Admin, Performance Admin, or HRBP can create and manage a Performance Improvement Plan for an employee — a time-boxed set of improvement objectives with success criteria, a review cadence, and an owning L1 Manager, typically (but not necessarily) originating from a low review outcome. The PIP runs independently of the appraisal cycle and has its own lifecycle. Data: performance_improvement_plans (§4.10.19).

**User story: **As an HRBP, I want to place an underperforming employee on a structured improvement plan with measurable objectives and a defined review cadence, so progress is tracked to a documented outcome.

**Acceptance criteria:**

- PIP is created against an employee (§4.10.19); it may optionally link to the originating review_record.

- Objectives: one or more { objective, success_criteria, weightage }; weightage across objectives must sum to 100% (VAL-WSUM-PIP).

- Status lifecycle: DRAFT → ACTIVE → (EXTENDED) → COMPLETED_SUCCESS / COMPLETED_FAIL / CANCELLED.

- Review cadence (WEEKLY / FORTNIGHTLY / MONTHLY) drives PIP_REVIEW_DUE reminders to the owner and employee.

- The owning L1 Manager records dated progress notes; HR Admin / Performance Admin / HRBP can edit the plan.

- On closure, an outcome (SUCCESS / FAIL / EXTENDED) and notes are mandatory.

- Notifications: PIP_CREATED, PIP_REVIEW_DUE, PIP_CLOSED (§9.7).

**Business rules:**

- Restricted to HR Admin, Performance Admin, and HRBP (performance.pip.manage, §3.7.5).

- A PIP is independent of goal_plans / review_cycles state; closing a review cycle does not close an open PIP.

- Outcome COMPLETED_FAIL does not auto-separate the employee; it surfaces a recommended action for HR Admin (separation handled in M03, referenced).

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Employee separates during an ACTIVE PIP: the PIP is auto-CANCELLED with an audit note.

- Only one non-terminal PIP per employee; creating a new PIP requires the prior one to be in a terminal state.

**FR-M09-019: Goal score computation**

**Description: **Goal score is computed per goal using a fixed formula. Overall goal rating for the plan is a weighted aggregate. Achievement is capped at 120% for scoring purposes (raw achievement % displayed without cap on the goal card).

**User story: ***As an L1 Manager, I want goal scores to be computed predictably from achievement % and weightage so that I can defend ratings to my reportees and to calibration.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- **Per-goal score formula:** goal_score = min(goal_achievement_pct, 120) / 100 × goal_weightage. Result is a decimal value.

- **Overall goal rating formula: overall_goal_score = sum(goal_score for all goals in plan) / sum(goal_weightage for all goals in plan). Every goal on the plan is rated during the appraisal review period. Mapped to the configured rating_scales for the cycle.**

- **Sub-goal handling:** Sub-goals (KRs) are informational. The parent goal’s goal_achievement_pct is entered manually by the employee — NOT auto-rolled-up from sub-goals (matches FR-M09-002).

- **Sub-goal weightage rule:** Sub-goal weightages must sum to 100% within their parent goal. Validation enforced at sub-goal save.

- **Achievement cap:** Configurable per tenant via Score Validations - Cap Achievement% for score flag (source: GoalPlanKraSettings). Default cap = 120%. Cap applies only to scoring; raw % is preserved for display and reporting.

- Goal score and overall score are computed at goal save (real-time) and at review evaluation; both employee and L1 see live scores during execution and at review.

**Business rules:**

- Resolves PN-PF02 (Custom Formula) by establishing the platform default. Tenants can still override via the custom formula framework (Phase 2 — PN-PF02 deferred for custom logic).

- **Plan weightage = 100% — hard enforcement, no bypass. sum(goal_weightage) == 100 is enforced at TWO checkpoints: (1) employee plan submission (approval_state DRAFT → PENDING_APPROVAL) is blocked if sum != 100; (2) L1 Manager approval action is blocked if sum != 100. No role — including HR Admin, Performance Admin, or Platform Super Admin — can bypass this rule. Plans with sum != 100 cannot exist in approval_state = APPROVED.**

- Employee can save a draft (approval_state = DRAFT) with any sum during composition; the validation fires only on submit-for-approval. During draft, the plan page shows a live “Total weightage: X% (need 100%)” indicator with the delta.

- All goals on the plan are counted toward sum(goal_weightage) for the 100% validation and are included in overall_goal_score once rated during the appraisal review period.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Goal with goal_achievement_pct = 0 contributes 0 to numerator, full weightage to denominator (denominator is always 100 by the hard enforcement rule above).

- Goal with goal_achievement_pct = 150 (overachievement) scores as if it were 120% per cap; raw 150% retained for display.

- Plan with no terminal-state goals at cycle close → overall_goal_score = NULL and audit-log flag; HR Admin reviews case-by-case.

- Submission attempt with sum != 100: blocked with explicit error listing each goal with its weight and the current total; submit button is disabled until sum equals 100. No override available.

- L1 approval attempt on a plan with sum != 100 (only possible if validation drifted due to a goal deletion during approval window): approval blocked with same error and the goal that broke the sum highlighted. Plan returns to employee for rebalance.

- Goal deletion that would break the 100% rule: deletion is permitted but plan immediately moves to weightage_imbalanced = TRUE flag state — employee notified to rebalance; goal cannot be approved until balance restored.

**FR-M09-021: SLA settings ****&**** escalation**

**Description: **SLA durations for performance tasks are configurable platform-wide. HR Admin and Performance Admin both have edit rights. SLA breach triggers escalation to the next-level manager and to HR Admin. Resolves locked decisions DM-PF02 and PN-PF04.

**User story: ***As a Performance Admin, I want to configure SLA durations for each performance task type so that breaches automatically escalate and reviewers don’t sit on overdue tasks indefinitely.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- SLA configuration screen accessible to **HR Admin AND Performance Admin** (joint capability per role).

- Configurable SLA durations (defaults in business days):

| Task type | Default SLA | Configurable range |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Performance approval requests | 3 days | 1–10 days |
| Self review | 5 days | 3–10 days |
| Evaluation task (L1) | 7 days | 5–15 days |
| Calibration | 10 days | 5–20 days |
| Acknowledgement | 5 days | 3–10 days |
| New goal plan assigned task | 5 days | 3–10 days |

- Escalation path on SLA breach: notify L2 Manager + HR Admin in-app and email, with explicit “SLA breached by X days” subject.

- Changes to SLA settings are audit-logged with changed_by_user_id, change_reason (optional), effective_from (defaults to immediate).

- Existing in-flight tasks continue under the SLA that was in effect when the task was created; new SLA applies to tasks initiated after the change.

**Business rules:**

- SLAs are counted in business days excluding weekly offs and holidays (per employee’s location calendar).

- Escalation does NOT auto-action the task; it only notifies. The task remains with its original owner unless manually reassigned.

- Multiple escalation tiers: tier 1 at SLA + 0 days (initial breach), tier 2 at SLA + 3 days (re-escalation), tier 3 at SLA + 7 days (HR Admin must intervene).

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Task initiated during a holiday/weekend window — SLA clock starts on the next business day.

- L2 Manager unavailable (separated, on leave ≥ 15 days) — escalation routes directly to HR Admin with audit-log note.

- SLA change made mid-cycle — affected tasks list shown to admin before save; admin confirms which tasks to migrate to new SLA.

**FR-M09-022: Manual calibration and publish**

**Description: **Calibration and publish are manual actions performed by HR Admin or Performance Admin. No automated publish on calibration completion — publish is a separate explicit action. Either role can perform either action (joint capability).

**User story: ***As an HR Admin, I want explicit manual control over calibration and publish so that I can hold ratings until all calibration sessions across the org are complete, and publish to all employees at once.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Calibration action: HR Admin or Performance Admin opens the calibration page for a cycle, reviews draft_rating for each employee, and sets calibration_rating. Save action commits calibration but does NOT publish.

- Publish action: HR Admin or Performance Admin clicks an explicit “Publish ratings to employees” button. On publish, calibration_rating is copied to published_rating and the review record moves to Acknowledgement stage.

- Both calibrate and publish actions are audit-logged with actor user_id and timestamp.

- HR Admin and Performance Admin both see the same calibration and publish screens with identical capabilities.

- Publish can be performed for a whole cycle (all employees at once) or scoped to a specific group (by department, manager, or grade) for staggered rollout.

**Business rules:**

- Resolves locked decision DM-PF12 (calibration mandatoriness) operationally — calibration is mandatory and manual.

- Publish is irreversible without HR Admin un-publish action (which is audit-logged and triggers a re-acknowledgement requirement).

- A cycle cannot move from Calibration to Closed without publish — publish gates closure.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Publish attempted before all employees in the cycle have a calibration_rating set: publish blocked with explicit list of pending employees; HR Admin can choose to exclude pending employees (audit-logged) or wait.

- Concurrent publish action by HR Admin and Performance Admin: first action wins, second action sees “Already published” message with timestamp of first publish.

- Publish reverted via un-publish: employees who already acknowledged are notified; their acknowledgement is preserved but flagged “pre-revision” in audit.

**FR-M09-023: Calibration ideal distribution norm**

**Description: **Calibration moderators (HR Admin / Performance Admin) see an ideal rating distribution norm as a benchmark when calibrating. Default: bell curve 15% top tier / 70% middle tier / 15% bottom tier. HRBP can override on a per-cycle basis with audit-log. Resolves locked decisions DM-PF03 and PN-PF05.

**User story: ***As an HR Admin running calibration, I want to see how my draft ratings compare to an ideal bell curve so that I can spot rating inflation or excessive harshness before publishing.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Calibration page displays a live distribution chart: actual draft_rating distribution vs ideal norm.

- Default ideal distribution: top 15% (highest tier of rating scale) · middle 70% · bottom 15% (lowest tier).

- Distribution norm configurable per cycle by HR Admin or Performance Admin; configurable values per rating-scale tier must sum to 100%.

- Norm is a benchmark, not a forced quota — calibrator can publish ratings that do not match the norm; visual indicator flags significant deviation (> 10 percentage points from norm).

- HRBP override on a per-cycle basis: HRBP can adjust the norm for cycles affecting their employee scope; override audit-logged with override_reason.

**Business rules:**

- Distribution norm applies per rating scope (cycle-wide, or per business unit, or per grade — configurable per calibration_configurations).

- For rating scales with more than 3 tiers, distribution is configurable per tier (e.g. 5-tier scale: 5% / 15% / 60% / 15% / 5%).

- HRBP override does NOT replace HR Admin / Performance Admin’s calibrate authority — HRBP only adjusts the benchmark.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Distribution norm sums to ≠ 100 — save blocked with explicit error.

- Cohort size < 20 employees — norm enforcement effectively suppressed (sample too small for meaningful distribution); visual indicator hidden, audit-log flags small cohort.

- Norm overridden mid-cycle — original norm preserved in audit-log; calibrators see both old and new values for context.

**FR-M09-024: Cycle closure**

**Description: Cycle closure flow: acknowledgement SLA, auto-acknowledgement on SLA breach, goals becoming read-only when the cycle closes, separation handling for mid-cycle exits, and the employee dispute path post-acknowledgement.**

**User story: ***As a Performance Admin, I want a defined cycle closure sequence so that goals are properly archived, employees who haven’t acknowledged are not blocked indefinitely, and the next cycle starts cleanly.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Acknowledgement stage SLA: 5 business days from publish per FR-M09-021. Notification at day 0, day 3, day 5 (final reminder).

- Auto-acknowledgement on SLA breach: at day 5 + 1, review_records.acknowledged_at is set automatically with auto_acknowledged = TRUE flag and audit-log entry “Auto-acknowledged on SLA breach”.

- Goal closure: when review_cycles.state moves to CLOSED, all goals on plans tied to that cycle become read-only (system action).

- Mid-cycle separation handling: when an employee’s lifecycle_state (M01) moves to NOTICE or LAST_WORKING_DAY, their active plan’s review is initiated immediately by HR Admin with partial_cycle_flag = TRUE. Review goes through Self → L1 → Calibration → Publish using the same stages; published rating is included in M03 separation record.

- Dispute path: within 5 business days of acknowledgement (or auto-acknowledgement), employee can raise a dispute via Profile › Performance › Raise dispute. Dispute routes to HRBP (tier 1) → L2 Manager (tier 2) → HR Admin (tier 3). Resolves locked decision DM-PF05.

**Business rules:**

- Auto-acknowledgement preserves dispute rights — employee can still raise a dispute within 5 business days after auto-ack.

- Disputes raised after cycle is CLOSED are still accepted but routed to HR Admin only (no L1 Manager involvement; HR Admin can re-open the cycle if dispute is upheld).

- Goal closure is a one-way transition; re-opening a closed goal requires HR Admin override with audit-log entry.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Employee separated before acknowledgement: review record published with acknowledged_at = NULL and separated_pre_ack = TRUE; dispute rights expire on LWD.

- Cycle closure attempted while dispute is open: closure blocked; HR Admin must resolve the dispute first.

- Auto-acknowledged employee raises dispute within window: dispute proceeds normally; auto-ack flag retained in audit.

**FR-M09-025: L1 Manager appraisal review screen — goal-by-goal evaluation**

**Description: **Dedicated review screen for L1 Manager to evaluate a reportee’s goal plan during the L1 Evaluator stage of the appraisal cycle (FR-M09-003 stage 2). Each goal row displays the goal name, full description, **measurement criteria**, weightage, employee’s self-rating, employee’s self-comments, lifecycle state at cycle close, achievement %, computed goal score, and an evaluation panel for L1 to enter rating + comments. Panel-level overall comments and a stage action bar (Save draft · Sendback · Submit to calibration) sit at the bottom.

**User story: ***As an L1 Manager during the FY26 mid-year review, I want to see each of my reportee’s goals with the full context — what they committed to, how it was supposed to be measured, what they achieved — alongside their self-rating, so that I can evaluate fairly and document my reasoning per goal.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Screen layout: reportee summary banner at top (name · designation · band · current L1 = me · cycle name · review template) → goal-by-goal evaluation cards → overall comments section → action bar.

- **Per-goal evaluation card** displays:

- Header: goal name · weightage · scorecard pillar

- Goal description (full text, expandable)

- **Goal measurement (full text)** — surfaced prominently in a separate subsection so L1 has the original measurement criteria available while evaluating

- Achievement %: employee’s entered value · raw and capped at 120% for scoring

- Computed goal score (auto-calculated per FR-M09-019, read-only)

- Self-rating: employee’s chosen rating on configured scale · self-comments (read-only to L1)

- L1 evaluation panel: rating dropdown (configured scale) · L1 comments textarea (mandatory if rating differs from self-rating by ≥ 1 point) · evidence attachment (optional, up to 5 files)

- Per-goal save (auto-saves draft on field blur)

- Overall comments section: free-text mandatory L1 comments on the overall plan; minimum 50 characters; maximum 3,000 characters.

- Action bar (sticky bottom):

- **Save draft** — keeps review in IN_PROGRESS state, no notification

- **Sendback to employee** — returns to Self stage, mandatory sendback reason, discards L1 evaluation per FR-M09-016

- **Submit to calibration** — locks review record, moves to Calibration stage per FR-M09-022, notifies HR Admin + Performance Admin

- L1 can navigate between team members’ reviews via a left-rail list showing review status pills (Not started · In progress · Submitted · Sent back · Calibrated · Published).

- All ratings, comments, and edit history are audit-logged with timestamp and editor user_id.

**Business rules:**

- L1 cannot Submit-to-calibration until ALL goals on the plan have L1 rating + comments entered.

- L1 cannot Submit-to-calibration until overall comments meet the 50-character minimum.

- If employee’s plan has weightage_sum != 100 (e.g. drifted post-approval due to goal deletion), Submit is blocked with the same “Rebalance required” error from FR-M09-019; review remains in IN_PROGRESS until plan is restored.

- L1 sees the employee’s goal_measurement field as the original commitment. L1 cannot edit the measurement criteria during review (it’s the contract for the cycle).

- Every goal on the plan requires L1 evaluation during the appraisal review period — the L1 enters an achievement assessment and rating for each goal.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Reportee on extended leave at review time: review record can be created with evaluated_during_leave = TRUE flag; calibration & publish proceed; acknowledgement SLA extended per FR-M09-024 separation handling logic.

- Reportee transferred mid-cycle: goals persist with the employee and the current L1 evaluates the full cycle as the inheriting manager; prior L1’s notes (if any in goals_history) are surfaced as reference but the evaluation is the new L1’s responsibility.

- Browser navigation away with unsaved draft: auto-save on blur prevents data loss; explicit confirmation dialog if user attempts to leave with > 30s of unsaved input.

- Two L1 sessions open concurrently: last-write-wins on per-field save; conflicting save shows “Refresh and retry” with field-level diff.

**FR-M09-026: Continuous manager feedback within appraisal cycle**

**Description: L1 Manager can deliver written feedback to any direct reportee at any time during the active appraisal cycle, independent of the formal review-cycle stage (Self, L1, Calibration, Acknowledgement, etc.). Feedback is captured in a persistent thread on the reportee’s appraisal record and is visible to the employee in real time. This is the continuous-feedback channel — distinct from the gated manager_comments_text and manager_rating fields that L1 fills only during the L1 review stage at cycle end.**

**User story: ***As an L1 Manager, I want to give my reportees feedback as observations happen — not wait for the formal end-of-cycle review — so coaching is timely, behavioural patterns are addressed early, and the formal review has a documented trail of touchpoints to draw on rather than relying on memory.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Feedback action is available on the L1 Manager’s reportee detail screen (FR-M01-005 manager-scoped view) and on the My Goals page of the reportee when accessed by the manager. Trigger: “Add feedback” button. Form fields: feedback_text (max 4000 chars, required) · feedback_category (enum: POSITIVE, CONSTRUCTIVE, COACHING, RECOGNITION) · visibility (enum: EMPLOYEE_VISIBLE — default, HRBP_ONLY — for sensitive concerns).

- Feedback is created in the manager_feedback entity. Schema: id (uuid) · employee_id (uuid) · author_user_id (uuid, the L1 Manager) · goal_plan_id (uuid, the ACTIVE plan at time of creation) · feedback_text (string 4000) · feedback_category (enum) · visibility (enum) · created_at (timestamp UTC) · updated_at (timestamp UTC) · is_edited (boolean, default FALSE). The L1 Manager can edit their own feedback within 24 hours of creation; after 24 hours feedback is frozen (an addendum can be added as a new entry).

- Feedback is visible to: the employee (if visibility = EMPLOYEE_VISIBLE), the L1 Manager (author), the HRBP, and HR Admin. Skip-level L2 has read access. Other managers do not see it.

- Feedback persists across appraisal cycles. When the current goal_plan transitions to CLOSED, prior feedback remains accessible from the employee’s historical performance tab; new feedback associates to the next ACTIVE plan.

- Email notification to employee (if visibility = EMPLOYEE_VISIBLE) at creation; in-app notification badge appears on the employee’s dashboard until viewed.

**Business rules:**

- No rate limit on number of feedback entries per cycle. Feedback during the L1 review stage (post plan_end_date) is permitted but flagged with a created_during_review = TRUE marker.

- Feedback cannot be deleted by the L1 Manager once submitted. Retraction is via HR Admin with audit (DM12 sensitive-change pattern).

- The L1 review stage’s formal manager_comments_text (FR-M09-019, FR-M09-025) is distinct from continuous feedback and remains required at L1 review completion regardless of how many continuous feedback entries exist.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Reportee transferred to a new L1 mid-cycle: prior L1’s feedback remains visible to the new L1 (read-only). New L1 can add new feedback. Both sets surface on the employee’s record.

- Reportee on extended leave: feedback creation is allowed; employee receives the in-app notification on return. No SLA on employee response.

- L1 Manager separated mid-cycle: their feedback entries are preserved; visibility unchanged. The acting manager (per HOD reassignment) can add new feedback.

**FR-M09-027: Employee response to manager feedback**

**Description: **Employee can write a response to any feedback entry their L1 Manager has added on their appraisal record. Responses form a two-way thread, are visible to the same audience as the original feedback (employee, L1, HRBP, HR Admin, L2 read-only), and are part of the persistent appraisal trail.

**User story: ***As an employee, I want to respond to my manager’s feedback — to confirm my understanding, share my perspective, or note context my manager may not have seen — so the appraisal trail captures both sides of a coaching conversation, not just the manager’s view.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- On the employee’s My Goals or Feedback tab, each feedback entry from the L1 Manager (with visibility = EMPLOYEE_VISIBLE) displays a “Respond” action. Form fields: response_text (max 4000 chars, required) · response_type (enum: ACKNOWLEDGE, ADD_CONTEXT, RESPECTFULLY_DISAGREE).

- Responses are stored in the feedback_responses entity. Schema: id (uuid) · feedback_id (uuid, FK → manager_feedback.id) · employee_id (uuid) · response_text (string 4000) · response_type (enum) · created_at (timestamp UTC) · updated_at (timestamp UTC) · is_edited (boolean, default FALSE). Employee can edit their own response within 24 hours of creation; after 24 hours the response is frozen (a follow-up response can be added as a new entry).

- Multiple responses per feedback are permitted (employee can respond, manager can reply with new feedback, employee can respond again — true conversation thread). Responses render in chronological order under each feedback entry.

- Employee receives no SLA pressure to respond; responding is optional. The thread state per feedback entry has three labels: NO_RESPONSE_YET (default), EMPLOYEE_ACKNOWLEDGED (if any response of type ACKNOWLEDGE exists), EMPLOYEE_RESPONDED (if any response exists). Visible in the manager’s view of the thread.

- Email notification to L1 Manager and HRBP at response creation; in-app notification appears on the L1’s dashboard until viewed.

- Responses persist across cycles alongside the feedback they belong to (same persistence as FR-M09-026).

**Business rules:**

- HRBP-only feedback (visibility = HRBP_ONLY per FR-M09-026) is NOT visible to the employee and cannot be responded to.

- Employee cannot delete their own response once submitted. Retraction is via HR Admin with audit (DM12 sensitive-change pattern).

- Responses do not change the original feedback. The feedback entry remains immutable (post 24-hour edit window).

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Feedback flagged as RESPECTFULLY_DISAGREE response: HRBP receives a real-time alert in addition to the L1 notification. HRBP can choose to intervene in the thread (with their own comment) or let the L1–employee conversation play out.

- Employee on extended leave: response action is available; no SLA.

- Manager edits their own feedback within the 24h window after employee has responded: response remains attached to the (now-edited) feedback. Edit audit-trail visible to both parties.

### FR-M09-006 — Goal Plan Template Builder

**Description. Tenant admins can build goal-plan templates via UI without code. Templates control structure of goal plans assigned to employees including field configurations, goal limits per category, weightage rules, sub-goal nesting, activity object attachment, and approval flow. Templates are versioned and bound to review cycles or UAGs.**

**Trigger.** Admin creates / edits / activates goal plan template.

**Inputs.** Template configuration via goal_plan_templates entity, goals_field_config entries, sub_goals_field_config entries.

**Processing.**

- Admin enters template metadata.

- Admin configures plan-level toggles (min/max goals, weightage rules, category limits).

- Admin selects which goal-level fields appear and their per-persona permissions.

- Admin optionally configures sub-goal fields.

- Admin selects approval workflow.

- Admin previews template using sample employee data.

- On activation, template made available for assignment.

- Version increments on edit; existing assigned plans retain their version.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- All fields from goal_plan_templates entity configurable in UI.

- Preview reflects exact employee experience.

- Versioning preserves historical plan integrity.

- Template clone-and-modify supported.

### FR-M09-007 — Review Template Builder

**Description.** Tenant admins build review templates via UI. Templates define cycle aliases, stage enablement, parameter inclusion, rating scale, visibility rules, workflow, reminders, normalization, and calibration.

**Trigger.** Admin creates / edits / activates review template.

**Inputs.** Template metadata, stage selections, parameter selections, alias overrides, rating scale, workflow.

**Processing.**

- Admin enters template metadata.

- Admin enables stages and orders them.

- Admin sets per-stage labels (cycle aliases).

- Admin selects parameters (Goals, Competencies, KPIs, OKRs, 360°, Skills, IDP, Comments).

- Admin configures per-stage × per-field permissions via review_stage_field_permissions.

- Admin selects rating scale, normalization rule, calibration config.

- Admin sets reminders, escalations, and workflow.

- On activation, template available for cycle binding.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- Stage order reorderable via drag-and-drop.

- Permission matrix editable without leaving builder.

- Preview shows reviewer’s exact form.

- Template clone-and-modify supported.

### FR-M09-008 — Per-Stage Field Permissions

**Description.** For each review stage, field-level controls determine which personas (Employee, Manager, HRBP, Admin) can view, edit, or are required to fill specific fields. Drives stage-specific UI rendering.

**Trigger.** Review template configuration, review submission.

**Inputs.** review_stage_field_permissions entries, current stage, current persona, field code.

**Processing.**

- On review form render for a stage, query permissions for current stage and persona.

- Hide fields with visible_to_persona[persona] = false.

- Disable editing on fields with editable_by_persona[persona] = false.

- Mark fields with mandatory_for_persona[persona] = true as required.

- Display prior-stage values when previous_stage_value_visible[stage] = true.

- Apply min/max length and character validation on save.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- Permissions matrix exactly mirrors UI rendering.

- Mandatory field violations prevent submission with clear error.

- Persona resolution accounts for role overlay (Manager × HRBP).

### FR-M09-009 — Calibration Moderation Page

**Description.** Facilitator-led moderation session UI displaying employee panel with configurable data (ratings, photos, goal progress, tenure, etc.) and supporting rating overrides with reason capture.

**Trigger.** Calibration session start (sync) or async deadline open.

**Inputs.** Calibration config, participant list, employees to calibrate, prior-stage ratings.

**Processing.**

- Load configured moderation page columns from calibration_configurations.

- Display per-employee row with enabled fields.

- Allow facilitator to filter by department / location / rating.

- Permit rating overrides with mandatory reason.

- Capture audit trail of every change.

- On session close, prompt distribution check vs ideal target.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- Loads under 5 seconds for 100-employee session.

- Overrides logged with reason and timestamp.

- Distribution diff visible at session close.

Post-calibration finalisation. After calibration is locked, each appraisee proceeds through final-rating release and employee acknowledgement before the cycle closes. The calibration page exposes per-appraisee post-stage fields: employee_acknowledgement (PENDING · ACKNOWLEDGED · ACKNOWLEDGED_WITH_COMMENTS · DISAGREED, with date and comments), and post_calibration_status (AWAITING_ACKNOWLEDGEMENT · CLOSED). These fields are visible to HR Admin, Performance Admin, the L1 Manager, the HOD, and the appraisee (their own record).

### FR-M09-010 — Normalization

**Description.** Apply bell-curve, ideal-distribution, Z-score, or percentile normalization to ratings within a population basis (department, location, grade, UAG, global).

**Trigger.** Pre-calibration cron or manual trigger by HR admin.

**Inputs.** Raw ratings, normalization rule, population segmentation criteria.

**Processing.**

- Group employees by normalization_basis.

- Skip groups below min_population_for_normalization.

- Apply normalization formula per rule_type.

- Persist normalized ratings as separate fields (raw rating preserved).

- Mark for calibration override.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- Raw and normalized values both visible to facilitator.

- Population threshold respected.

- Normalization audit log per employee.

### FR-M09-011 — Custom Formula Engine

**Description.** Allow tenants to define custom formulas for overall rating, eligibility, weighted averages, and promotion indices. Formulas evaluated via safe expression evaluator.

**Trigger.** Review form save, eligibility check, scorecard computation.

**Inputs.** custom_formulas entries, employee review data.

**Processing.**

- Parse formula expression.

- Resolve referenced field values from review context.

- Evaluate with division-by-zero and overflow protection.

- Cache computed result; invalidate on referenced-field change.

- Display formula output and intermediate values for transparency.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- Formula supports +, −, ×, ÷, parentheses, comparison operators.

- Safe evaluator prevents code injection.

- Division by zero handled gracefully.

- Formula errors surfaced clearly to admin.

### FR-M09-013 — AI Goal Generation

**Description.** Generate goal suggestions for an employee based on role, prior goals, competency framework, and organizational priorities, via integrated AI service.

**Trigger.** Employee or manager clicks “Suggest Goals” in plan builder.

**Inputs.** Employee’s role/JD, competency framework, prior cycle goals, organizational priorities.

**Processing.**

- Aggregate context per ai_goal_role_context_source.

- Send anonymized context to AI service.

- Receive suggestion list (up to ai_goal_max_suggestions_per_request).

- Display suggestions with rationale.

- User selects to accept, modify, or reject.

- Accepted suggestions added to plan as draft.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- No PII transmitted to AI service.

- Suggestions diverse across categories.

- User can regenerate with feedback.

- Audit log records suggestion acceptance.

**Service contract. **Endpoint POST /api/v1/performance/ai/suggest-goals (§6.4.7). Request: { employee_id, role_context, prior_goal_ids[], org_priority_ids[], count }. Response: array of { goal_name, measurement, suggested_weightage, scorecard_pillar, rationale }; the user selects suggestions to add (no auto-commit). NFR: p95 latency — 8s; on timeout or service failure the plan builder falls back to manual goal entry with a non-blocking notice. Privacy: only role / competency / prior-goal / org-priority context is sent — no compensation or PII-tier fields.

### FR-M09-014 — Cascade Reject-by-Employee

**Description.** Manager rejecting a cascaded goal can route it back to the employee for redrafting rather than fully rejecting, preserving the cascade chain.

**Trigger.** Manager rejects cascaded goal in plan review.

**Inputs.** Cascaded goal, rejection reason, redraft option.

**Processing.**

- On rejection, present manager with two options: REJECT (remove from plan) or RETURN_FOR_REDRAFT.

- If RETURN_FOR_REDRAFT, route goal back to employee with manager’s notes.

- Employee redrafts and resubmits.

- Cycle continues until accepted or full rejection.

**Acceptance criteria.**

- Loop iterations capped by max_send_back_iterations from review_templates.

- Audit log captures each iteration.

- Cascade chain preserved through iterations.

### M09 — Functional Specification v1 (Reference Source: M09_Performance_Management_Requirements.docx)

**Spec status.** Authored per the module Requirements Specification (canonical Part A–N template). All locked decisions from the source spec’s Part L are reproduced verbatim below as the contractual rules. Configuration values and approval flows are summarised; full configuration registers remain in the source spec.

**Module boundary:** M09 owns the performance appraisal lifecycle: goal plan configuration, monthly review cycle initiation, multi-stage evaluation workflow (Self → L1 Evaluator → Calibration → Acknowledgement), calibration moderation, Multi-Source Feedback collection, and rating publication. M09 publishes ratings to M10 Hikes & Compensation (Phase 2) and to M16 Analytics. M09 does NOT own goal achievement tracking (M01 employee record), compensation decisions (M10), or competency assessments (out of Phase 1 scope — see Part G).

**Decision-code namespace.** LC / DM-PF series. 47 decisions catalogued.

