## 5.9 M11 — Document Management

**FR-M11-001: Policy library with versioning and acknowledgement tracking**

**Description: **HR policy documents stored with version history (up to 10 versions on hot storage). Acknowledgement tracking with sign-off engine. Visibility cron recomputes every 24 hours.

**User story: ***As a Document Admin, I want a structured policy library with version control and acknowledgement tracking so that compliance audits can demonstrate which employees have acknowledged which policy versions.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Policies created via Document Admin UI with category, applicability rules, and visibility scope.

- Each new version creates a new policy_documents row (or updates current_version). Old versions remain queryable; up to 10 versions on hot storage, older versions archived to cold storage.

- Sign-off engine creates signoff_transactions rows for each in-scope employee on policy publication.

- Employees see a ‘My Policies’ app surface with pending acknowledgements highlighted.

**Business rules:**

- Visibility cron recomputes every 24 hours — drives lag between visibility change and employee view.

- Sign-off cron fires every 12 hours — recomputes pending-task lists and fires reminder notifications.

- Per-policy acknowledgement text override is disabled in Phase 1 — single global text applies.

- Allow decline configurable per policy — if enabled, decline reason is mandatory.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If an employee declines acknowledgement, manager and HR are notified per the decline reason prompt.

- Policy updates do not automatically supersede prior acknowledgements — employees must re-acknowledge the new version.

**FR-M11-002: Storage with encryption and pre-signed URLs**

**Description: **S3-equivalent object storage with bucket-level versioning. AES-256 encryption with KMS-managed per-tenant keys. TLS 1.3 mandatory in transit. Pre-signed URLs for download with 5-minute expiry. India home region with DR replica in second Indian region.

**User story: ***As a CTO, I want storage with versioning at the bucket layer plus application-layer metadata so that the version chain is preserved even if M11 metadata is rebuilt.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Documents written to S3-equivalent backend with versioning ON.

- Per-tenant KMS-managed encryption keys.

- Pre-signed download URLs expire after 5 minutes.

- Direct writes to the backend are blocked at the IAM layer — all writes go through the M11 storage adapter.

**Business rules:**

- Cold storage tier auto-transitions documents at version > 10 or post-retention legal-hold; cold-tier requires admin-initiated restore (up to 12 hours).

- Geographic residency: India home region; DR replica in a second Indian region.

- Maximum file size per upload: 25 MB.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If virus scan returns INFECTED, file is quarantined and not committed to durable storage.

- Pre-signed URL expiry creates download UX requirement — UI must refresh the link if the user keeps the page open beyond 5 minutes.

**FR-M11-003: Letter templates with merge fields**

**Description: **Letter templates managed via Letter Admin capability flag. Merge fields bind to employee/offer/separation context. E-signature: HR Admin signature on standard letters; CEO signature on offer and separation letters.

**User story: ***As a Letter Admin, I want to author and publish letter templates with merge fields so that operational letters can be generated consistently without manual editing.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Letter templates entity captures template body with {{merge_field}} placeholders.

- Merge fields supported: employee fields (name, designation, DOJ), offer fields (CTC, joining date), separation fields (LWD, reason).

- Template versioning supported — current_version increments on publish.

- Letter generation event: M02, M03, M08, M09 invoke M11 render-template API with employee context to produce a personalised PDF.

**Business rules:**

- E-signature default: HR Admin. Offer and Separation letters: CEO signature per migrated lock.

- Acknowledgement required by default — letter generates a signoff_transactions row for the recipient.

- Allow employee download of letters = Yes (carry forward).

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a merge field references data the employee record does not have, the render API returns an error with the missing fields enumerated.

- Template deprecation does not affect already-generated letters — those are stored as immutable PDFs.

**FR-M11-004: Form tagging on policies**

**Description: **When enabled, a configured form can be attached to a policy. Form is presented to employee after the policy preview and before the sign-off button. Form response is appended to the policy document for both user and admin views.

**User story: ***As a Document Admin, I want to attach forms to policies (e.g. emergency contact form to the safety policy) so that policy acknowledgement also captures necessary employee declarations.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- policy_documents.form_tagging_enabled = TRUE and form_template_id reference a form template.

- Sign-off UI shows policy → form → sign-off button sequence.

- Form response captured in form_responses entity; reference stored on signoff_transactions.form_response_id.

- Form response is appended to the policy document view for both user (own response) and admin (any response).

**Business rules:**

- Form fields configurable: text, single-select, multi-select, date, file upload.

- Form is mandatory if attached — submitting the policy sign-off without the form is blocked.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If form schema changes after some employees have submitted, prior responses are preserved as-is; new submissions follow the new schema.

**FR-M11-005: HR letter templates ****&**** generation**

**Description: **Letter templates for Appointment, Confirmation, Hike/Increment, Promotion, Reward, Bond, Experience, Salary Certificate, Employment Letter (visa/loan), Address-proof Letter. Templates carry merge fields populated at generation time from M01 (employee), M06 (compensation, Phase 2 placeholder), M09 (review). Letters carry an embedded digital signature from HR Admin or HOD.

**User story: ***As HR Admin, I want letter templates with merge fields so that I can generate a personalised letter for any employee in seconds without retyping fields, and so that letters carry a consistent format and signature across the company.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Phase 1 template catalogue: Appointment, Confirmation, Hike/Increment (FY-scoped), Promotion, Reward/Spot-bonus, Bond, Experience (on separation), Salary Certificate, Employment Letter, Address-proof Letter.

- Each template defines: name, body (rich text with {merge_field} placeholders), eligible signatories (HR Admin / HOD / CEO), retention period (default 7 years per DPDPA), default CC list.

- Merge fields resolve at generation time from M01 (name, designation, DOJ, location, manager), M06 stub (CTC, components), M09 (latest rating).

- Generated PDF embeds the digital signature image of the signatory per M11 DM12.

**Business rules:**

- Template edits require Document Admin role with audit log of changes; existing generated letters retain the template version they were generated from (versioned template).

- Per RBAC: HR Admin can generate any letter; HOD can generate only Reward, Experience for their reportees.

- Employee-initiated letter requests (Salary Certificate, Employment Letter, Address-proof) route to HR Admin queue with SLA: 2 business days for salary/address-proof, 3 business days for employment letter, 5 business days for experience letter.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Merge field unresolved (e.g. CTC pulled but M06 not yet live) → letter generation blocked with explicit list of missing fields.

- Template marked Inactive → existing letters readable; new generations from that template rejected.

**FR-M11-006: Official documents cluster catalogue**

**Description: **Document clusters organise the official documents an employee signs, declares, or has nominated: Sign-off cluster (PrimeSoft NDA, NDA Annexure, Client NDA), Statutory cluster (Gratuity Declaration, PF Form 11), Benefits cluster (Insurance Nomination — Personal Accident, Life Insurance Nomination), Compliance cluster (Signed Bond, Letter of Authorization), Onboarding cluster (Joining Report). Surfaced on Employee Profile › Official documents tab.

**User story: ***As HR Admin, I want all official documents an employee has signed grouped by cluster so that I can check completeness at a glance and chase missing ones; as an Employee, I want to view my full signed-document set without raising a ticket.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Cluster definitions in Phase 1: Sign-off, Statutory, Benefits, Compliance, Onboarding.

- Each cluster has a configured document list; documents marked Mandatory must be signed before onboarding completion (per M02 §A.2).

- Client NDA is a multi-instance document — multiple Client NDAs per employee, one per client project.

- Bond is N/A for senior hires (configured per offer in M08); profile shows N/A status with explanation pill.

- Documents stored in M11 vault per DM06/DM08 (size, virus-scan), retained 7 years per DM14.

**Business rules:**

- Document state: Signed (with timestamp + signatory), Filed (system-generated, no employee signature), Pending (assigned but not yet signed), N/A (not applicable).

- Visibility: Self always; L1 Manager — Joining Report only; HRBP & HR Admin — full set; Finance Admin — Statutory + Compliance clusters only.

- Missing-mandatory dashboard surfaces employees with Pending mandatory documents to HR Admin.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Document version replaced (e.g. NDA Annexure updated) → employees with the old version flagged for re-sign; new sign-off task generated.

- Cluster reorganisation (e.g. moving Bond from Compliance to Statutory) → historical employee records retain original cluster tag; only newly-signed instances follow the new cluster mapping.

## 5.10 M-CAL — Calendar (cross-module surface)

The Calendar module is a cross-module surface that aggregates events from M01 (holidays, birthdays, work anniversaries), M04 (approved and pending leave), M08 (interviews where the employee is an interviewer), and M11 (company events / town halls). It is presented in the operationally-relevant section of each role’s menu — never duplicated. Placement: Modules (Employee), Team (Manager L1), Department oversight (HOD), Modules — oversight (HR Admin), Modules — view (HRBP), Scoped operations (Office Admin), Statutory & FnF (Finance Admin), Operations (Onboarding Admin), Module ops (Leave / Attendance / Performance / Document Admins), Hiring (Recruiter), Hiring oversight (Recruitment Admin), Workspace (Org Admin). Calendar is not shown to Platform Super Admin or Candidates. Role-based scoping determines which events are visible.

**FR-CAL-001: Calendar module with multi-tab view**

**Description: **Calendar surfaces seven categories as tabs in a single view: Leaves, Holidays, Birthdays, Anniversaries (life events), Work anniversaries (employment milestones), Interviews, Events (company-wide). Each tab is independently filterable but the date-range selector applies globally.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want a single calendar view that shows everything happening around me — my team’s leaves, company holidays, my colleagues’ birthdays, work anniversaries, interviews I’m scheduled for, and company events — so that I have one place to check what’s coming up.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Calendar route surfaces seven tabs: Leaves, Holidays, Birthdays, Anniversaries, Work anniversaries, Interviews, Events.

- Each tab independently shows a count badge of events in the current window (e.g. “Leaves (2)”).

- Tab content displays a table of events with date, subject, supporting details, and (where applicable) a quick action (Send wishes, Celebrate, Apply for optional holiday, Open interview).

**Business rules:**

- Tab visibility is fixed in Phase 1 — all 7 tabs visible to all roles. Per-role tab hiding (e.g. hiding Interviews for non-interviewers) is a Phase 2 enhancement.

- Quick actions route to existing flows: Send wishes → FR-M09-recognition; Apply for optional holiday → M04 optional-holiday flow; Open interview → M08 interview detail.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Empty tab (no events in window) → “No events in this window” placeholder.

- Performance: server-side pagination at 100 events per tab; window scope automatically narrows if event count exceeds 500.

**FR-CAL-002: Default date window — current week**

**Description: **Calendar opens with a default window of the current week (Monday to Sunday of the week containing today). User can change to month, quarter, or a custom date range.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want the calendar to open showing this week by default so that the most immediately relevant events are visible without changing the window.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Default window on first open: Mon – Sun of the week containing the current date.

- Window indicator displayed prominently (e.g. “Current window: Mon 25 May – Sun 31 May 2026”).

- “This week” button resets to the current week from any other selection.

**Business rules:**

- Week start is platform-configurable (default Monday, configurable via Org Admin per locale).

- “Today” is the user’s local time zone (employee’s location time zone from M01).

**FR-CAL-003: Date range pickers — week, month, quarter, custom**

**Description: **User can navigate the calendar window by quick-select (Week / Month / Quarter buttons), step navigation (Prev / Next), or custom date range via From / To date pickers.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want flexible date navigation so that I can quickly switch between this week’s view and a quarter-ahead view, and pick custom ranges for retrospectives or planning.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Quick-select: Week (7 days), Month (calendar month containing the from-date), Quarter (3-month period containing the from-date).

- Step navigation: Prev / Next moves by the currently-selected unit (week / month / quarter).

- Custom range: From / To date pickers; max span 90 days to bound query cost.

- “Previous selections” history persists the last 5 selected ranges per user; selectable from a small dropdown.

**Business rules:**

- Custom range > 90 days → reject with message; suggest narrowing.

- Range entirely in the past or entirely in the future is allowed (no constraint other than the 90-day cap).

**FR-CAL-004: Scope filters — Self / direct reports / team / function / all**

**Description: **Below each tab, scope filters allow users to narrow events to “Self (my own events)”, “My direct reports”, “My team (department)”, “My function (cross-team)”, or “All employees”. Selecting Self alone produces a personal-only calendar view.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want a “Self” filter so that I can quickly switch to a personal calendar showing only my own approved leaves, my birthday, my work anniversary, interviews I’m scheduled for, and events I’ve RSVP’d to — without the team noise.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Filter chips below the tab row: Self (my own events), My direct reports, My team (department), My function (cross-team), All employees.

- Default ON: Self (always), My direct reports (managers only), My team (all roles with a department mapping).

- Default OFF: My function, All employees (turned on explicitly when needed).

- Filters are union-style (events matching any selected scope are shown).

- Self events surfaced: own approved/pending leaves, own birthday, own work anniversary, own scheduled interviews (panellist or candidate-side as applicable), events the user has RSVP’d to.

- Privacy: Birthdays and Anniversaries (life events) of others are visible only if the colleague has opted in via Profile › Personal › Privacy. Opt-out events are hidden from all scopes (Self always shows own events regardless).

**Business rules:**

- HR Admin sees Self + All employees scopes by default.

- “My function” is the cross-team grouping defined in M01 (e.g. all of Engineering across pods).

- Self filter cannot be hidden by role configuration — it is always available.

**FR-CAL-005: Event sources ****&**** data freshness**

**Description: **Calendar data is computed from source modules: Leaves → M04 leave_applications (status = Approved or Pending). Holidays → M04 holiday_calendar (filtered by employee location). Birthdays → M01 employees.date_of_birth (day + month match). Anniversaries → M01 employees.marriage_anniversary (opt-in). Work anniversaries → M01 employees.date_of_joining. Interviews → M08 interview_panel (where current user is panellist). Events → M11 company_events (or M01 calendar).

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want the calendar to reflect what’s truly happening — accurate to the current data — so that I don’t miss an event because of stale data.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Calendar query is real-time; no caching beyond a 60-second TTL for performance.

- Refresh button on the page triggers a re-query.

- Each tab’s underlying source module is named in a small footer (“Sources: M01, M04, M08, M11”).

**Business rules:**

- Leave applications in status Rejected or Withdrawn do NOT appear on the calendar.

- Holidays in employee’s assigned location only; optional holidays appear with an Apply action.

- Interviews only where current user is on the panel (interviewer-side view); candidate-side view in M08.

**FR-CAL-006: Quick actions on calendar events**

**Description: **Each event row offers a context-appropriate quick action: Birthdays / Anniversaries → Send wishes (recognition); Work anniversaries → Celebrate (recognition + congrats note); Holidays → Apply for optional holiday (if optional); Interviews → Open interview detail; Events → RSVP.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want to take action on a calendar event without leaving the calendar so that sending wishes for a birthday or RSVP’ing to a town hall is a one-click experience.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Send wishes → opens FR-M09 recognition form pre-populated with recipient.

- Celebrate (work anniversary) → opens recognition form with a “Years of service” template.

- Apply for optional holiday → opens FR-M04-014 (optional holiday application) pre-populated with the holiday date.

- Open interview → routes to M08 interview detail page.

- RSVP → records Going / Not going / Maybe with auto-add to user’s personal calendar.

**FR-CAL-007: Role-based default scope**

**Description: **Calendar’s default scope is role-dependent: Employee defaults to My team; Manager L1 defaults to My direct reports + My team; HOD defaults to My function; HR Admin defaults to All employees; HRBP defaults to assigned employees only.

**Business rules:**

- Role defaults are platform-configurable per tenant by Org Admin.

- User selections override defaults and persist per user across sessions.

- Privacy filters (birthday opt-in, anniversary opt-in) override role scope — opt-out events are never shown regardless of role.

## 5.11 M16 — Analytics & Reports

**FR-M16-001: Role-scoped data visibility in analytics**

**Description: **Analytics is available to all users — data visible within analytics is governed entirely by each user’s own role entitlement and data scope, not by the Analytics Admin role. Managers see only their team. HRBPs see only their assigned employees. HR Admins see their entity.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want to see my own analytics widget without needing a special analytics grant so that personal insights are part of my self-service experience.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Analytics queries are filtered by the requestor’s data scope at query time (row-level security).

- Manager (L1) sees aggregate metrics scoped to direct and indirect reports.

- HRBP sees metrics scoped to directly assigned employees.

- HR Admin sees metrics scoped to their entity.

- Org Admin / Finance Admin / specialist admins see scoped per their role.

**Business rules:**

- Analytics Admin holds configuration rights only — creating report definitions and managing dashboard layouts.

- Analytics Admin is entity-scoped by default. Tenant-wide report configuration granted via individual entitlement by Org Admin.

- Both entity-scoped and tenant-scoped Analytics Admin configurations are valid and audit-logged.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a manager’s reporting chain changes (e.g. team transferred), analytics auto-rescopes on next query — no stale-team data shown.

- Drill-down from dashboard widgets is subject to the same RBAC as direct module navigation — clicking a link does not bypass RBAC.

**FR-M16-002: Pre-built reports for Phase 1**

**Description: **Phase 1 pre-built reports: Headcount Snapshot, 30-day and 90-day Attrition Trend, Leave Balance Summary, Attendance Summary, BGC Pending Actions, Open Requisitions, FnF Clearance Pending, Asset Returns Due.

**User story: ***As an HR Admin, I want pre-built reports out of the box so that I do not need to construct common HR queries from scratch.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- report_definitions seeded at provisioning with the Phase 1 pre-built set.

- Each report has a category, scope, refresh frequency, and viewer-role visibility.

- Reports execute at query time with viewer’s data scope filter applied.

- Custom report builder available to Analytics Admin / Org Admin.

**Business rules:**

- Report generation target: < 30 seconds for standard reports.

- Dashboard metrics requiring aggregation (headcount, attrition) are computed in background jobs and cached. Cache TTL configurable by Org Admin — default 4 hours.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a report’s data source is unavailable (e.g. external integration down), report surfaces a ‘data temporarily unavailable’ state with last-refreshed timestamp.

**FR-M16-003: Role-aware dashboard widgets**

**Description: **Dashboard is the first screen after login. Every widget is conditionally rendered based on role and data scope. PII protection ceilings apply even to aggregated display — salary, bank, government IDs, health data never shown on dashboard cards.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want a dashboard tailored to my role so that I see only what is relevant to me without manually navigating to multiple modules.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- dashboard_widgets entity defines per-role visibility.

- Widget visibility evaluated at page load.

- Empty state defined per widget — ‘No pending approvals’ rather than blank card or loading spinner.

- Pending approval count surfaced as numeric badge on navigation and widget.

**Business rules:**

- Compensation never shown on dashboard for any role, including Finance Admin and HR Admin — these require direct navigation to M10 (Phase 2) or M06 (Phase 2).

- Announcements shown in reverse chronological order. HR Admin can pin up to 3 announcements.

- Chat Agent is a persistent global element, not a dashboard widget — accessible from a persistent icon on all screens.

- Mobile parity (Phase 2 dependency): widgets must render at 375 px, 768 px, 1280 px breakpoints for Phase 1 web.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a widget has no data for a given user (e.g. Manager with no reports), empty state is shown — not the widget hidden.

- Birthday and Work Anniversary widget shows only first name and anniversary year — DOB never shown. Birthday sharing is employee opt-in (share_birthday_with_team, month + day only); Org Admin only enables/disables the widget org-wide.

**FR-M16-004: Custom report builder**

**Description: **Org Admin and Analytics Admin can define new report formats without developer involvement. Custom reports respect viewer’s data scope filtering.

**User story: ***As an Analytics Admin, I want to create custom report definitions so that I can address ad-hoc reporting needs without code deployment.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Custom report builder UI allows selecting fields from available entities, defining filters, and configuring presentation (table, chart).

- Custom report saved as a report_definitions row with report_type = CUSTOM.

- On execution, row-level security is applied — viewer sees only data within their entitlement.

- Custom reports can be scheduled for delivery (email, in-app).

**Business rules:**

- Sensitive fields (PII Tier 1, compensation) are not selectable in custom reports for roles without permitted access — selector hides them.

- Report sharing: custom reports can be shared with specific roles or users, audit-logged.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a custom report definition references a field that becomes disabled, the report surfaces a configuration error and the Analytics Admin is prompted to revise.

## 5.12 M17 — Assets & IT

**FR-M17-001: Asset master with category, condition, and lifecycle**

**Description: **Asset master holds type, make, serial number, value, condition, location. Condition tracked through lifecycle (NEW → GOOD → FAIR → POOR → DAMAGED → WRITTEN_OFF).

**User story: ***As an IT Admin, I want a structured asset master so that asset inventory is auditable and assignment is traceable.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- assets entity supports configurable asset categories.

- Unique asset_tag and unique serial_number (within asset_category) constraints enforced.

- Status workflow: AVAILABLE → ASSIGNED → AVAILABLE / IN_REPAIR / WRITTEN_OFF / LOST.

- Audit log captures every status transition and condition change.

**Business rules:**

- Asset categories: LAPTOP, DESKTOP, MOBILE, TABLET, MONITOR, ACCESSORY, ID_CARD, ACCESS_CARD, OTHER.

- Asset master maintained by IT Admin within their entity.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If an asset is reported lost, status is set LOST and a notification fires to IT Admin and HR Admin. Recovery flow allows status restoration to AVAILABLE.

**FR-M17-002: Asset assignment triggered from onboarding**

**Description: **Asset assignment is triggered automatically from the onboarding task list when IT Admin marks provisioning complete. Asset assignment creates an asset_assignments row.

**User story: ***As an IT Admin completing onboarding provisioning, I want asset assignment to flow automatically from the onboarding task so that I do not need to enter the assignment in two places.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- When an IT_PROVISIONING task is marked COMPLETED in M02, IT Admin selects the specific asset(s) to assign.

- asset_assignments row created with assignment_source = ONBOARDING, employee_id, asset_id, assigned_at = now.

- Asset status transitions AVAILABLE → ASSIGNED.

- Notification to employee confirms asset assignment.

**Business rules:**

- Multiple assets can be assigned per onboarding task — e.g. laptop + monitor + access card.

- Standalone assignment (post-onboarding) is also supported via IT Admin direct action.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If the assigned asset is unavailable at the moment of provisioning task completion, IT Admin selects an alternative; the task does not block on stock.

**FR-M17-003: Asset clearance — parallel stage in FnF workflow**

**Description: **Asset clearance is a parallel stage in the FnF workflow. FnF cannot complete until IT Admin records the return of all assigned assets.

**User story: ***As an IT Admin, I want asset clearance integrated into the FnF workflow so that no FnF settlement proceeds without confirmed asset returns.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- When M03 separation_records reaches LWD-7 (or configured), an fnf_clearances row is created for department = IT, assigned to IT Admin.

- Employee’s currently-assigned assets are listed in the IT clearance task.

- IT Admin marks each asset returned by updating asset_assignments.returned_at and return_condition.

- When all assets are returned, IT clearance status moves to CLEARED.

**Business rules:**

- Lost or unreturned assets are flagged with return_condition = LOST; the case is escalated to HR Admin for recovery action.

- Asset clearance status is one of multiple parallel clearances; FnF completion requires all departments to clear.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If the separating employee disputes an assigned asset (claims never received), IT Admin investigates and the dispute is audit-logged with resolution.

**FR-M17-004: Asset recovery and cost-deduction on damaged or unreturned assets**

**Description: **When an asset is returned damaged beyond repair, lost, or not returned at separation, IT Admin captures evidence and a recommended recovery value (per the depreciation schedule); HR Admin reviews and decides the recovery posture. The recovery recommendation feeds FnF settlement.

**User story: ***As an IT Admin, I want a structured recovery flow so that damaged or unreturned assets are escalated to HR with a recommended recovery value rather than written off silently.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- On return condition Damaged-beyond-repair or items missing, IT Admin captures photos, condition notes, and a recommended recovery value computed from purchase_cost and the class depreciation schedule.

- An asset clearance exception (assets.clearance_exception) is emitted to M03 with the exception code and recommended recovery value.

- HR Admin reviews the recovery recommendation and decides the recovery posture (waive, partial, or full recovery).

- On a recover decision, a recovery_recommended event is emitted to M06 Payroll (Phase 2) for FnF settlement adjustment; in Phase 1 the value is recorded against the FnF clearance record for manual handling.

**Business rules:**

- Recommended recovery value is advisory; HR Admin holds the final recovery decision.

- Recovery is the only point at which HR Admin engages with asset data — the HR employee record does not surface asset inventory by default.

- Lost assets follow the 30-day Lost → Written Off auto-rule (FR-M17-005) in parallel with recovery review.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If the separating employee disputes an assigned asset (claims never received), IT Admin investigates; the dispute and its resolution are audit-logged before any recovery proceeds.

- If recovery is waived, the asset is written off with reason captured and Finance Admin notified for booking.

**FR-M17-005: Asset lifecycle state machine with Lost → Written Off auto-transition**

**Description: **The asset master is governed by an eight-state lifecycle (In Stock · Reserved · In Use · Under Maintenance · Retired · Disposed · Lost · Written Off). A lost asset auto-transitions to Written Off 30 calendar days after entering the Lost state, notifying Finance Admin and triggering an insurance claim where a policy is attached.

**User story: ***As an IT Asset Manager, I want a governed asset state machine with an automatic Lost → Written Off rule so that unrecoverable assets are booked consistently without manual chasing.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Asset state is system-controlled via the allowed-transitions table; invalid transitions are rejected.

- Entering the Lost state auto-opens a loss-investigation ticket and notifies HR Admin; a 30-calendar-day recovery clock starts.

- At 30 days from Lost-state entry, the system auto-transitions Lost → Written Off, notifies Finance Admin for write-off booking, triggers an insurance claim for capitalised assets with an active policy, and auto-closes the loss-investigation ticket with disposition reason.

- Disposed and Written Off records are locked read-only; soft delete only — no hard deletes on any asset record.

- Every state change emits to the P05 Audit & Compliance Log within 5 seconds.

**Business rules:**

- Eight states: In Stock, Reserved, In Use, Under Maintenance, Retired, Disposed, Lost, Written Off.

- Reserved auto-reverts to In Stock after 14 days if no assignment completes.

- The 30-day Lost → Written Off window is tenant-configurable by the IT Asset Manager.

- Manual write-off (Retired / Under Maintenance → Written Off) requires joint IT Admin + Finance Admin sign-off — distinct from the auto-rule.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a Lost asset is recovered after the 30-day Written Off transition, the Written Off → In Use transition is blocked; IT Admin opens a reversal request requiring Finance Admin sign-off, and on approval the asset is re-onboarded as a new record (EX-ITSM-08).

- Back-dated transitions are allowed up to 30 days; beyond 30 days require Service Desk Admin approval and are logged as audit exceptions.

**FR-M17-006: Asset tagging, capitalisation thresholds, and annual audit certification**

**Description: **Tangible assets above the tagging threshold carry a physical ORG-{CAT}-{5d} tag distinct from the immutable internal AST-ID. Assets above the capitalisation threshold are capitalised and depreciated. An annual audit cycle has Department Heads certify their team's assigned assets.

**User story: ***As an IT Asset Manager, I want enforced tagging and an annual Department-Head-certified audit so that the asset register stays accurate and audit-ready.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Each asset carries an internal Asset ID (AST-{entity}-{6d}, immutable) and, for hardware above the tagging threshold, a physical Asset Tag (ORG-{CAT}-{5d}) linked 1:1.

- Assets with acquisition cost at or above the capitalisation threshold (default ₹15,000, Finance-configurable) are capitalised and feed depreciation; below threshold are expensed.

- Assets at or above the tagging threshold (default $100 / ₹8,500) must be tagged; below threshold are tracked as bulk stock (Accessories tier) with no per-asset record.

- Annual audit: IT Operations publishes the schedule in January; each Department Head receives an exported list of assets assigned to their team, verifies within 10 business days, and flags discrepancies; the IT Asset Manager signs off the reconciliation within a further 5 business days.

- Unresolved discrepancies after the 10-business-day window escalate to CTO; the asset is held with audit_exception_flag = true until resolved.

**Business rules:**

- The tagging threshold is distinct from the capitalisation threshold: every capitalised asset is tagged, but not every tagged asset is capitalised.

- Department Head audit-certify is a capability flag granted by default to every HOD at provisioning; HODs cannot edit asset records — flagging produces an audit-exception ticket to IT Admin.

- Lost or damaged physical tags must be reported within 5 business days; the IT Asset Manager re-issues a replacement tag retaining the same ORG number (the number is bound to the record, not the label).

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Assets reported missing during audit are flagged Lost; after 30 days without recovery they follow the Lost → Written Off auto-rule (FR-M17-005).

- If a tag is unreported and later discovered missing during audit, it is flagged as an audit exception (EX-ITSM-19).

**FR-M17-007: IT provisioning workflow consumed by M02 onboarding**

**Description: **M17 publishes a six-stage 'Asset Assignment' provisioning workflow that M02 Onboarding consumes for new joiners: bundle resolution, stock check, asset preparation, account provisioning, asset assignment, and task completion back to M02.

**User story: ***As an IT Admin, I want a structured provisioning workflow triggered from onboarding so that new joiners receive the right device bundle, accounts, and access by Day 1.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- On inbound m02.it_provisioning_task_assigned (emitted by M02 five working days before start date, lead time configurable per location), the system resolves the default device bundle for the joiner's role family and shows it to the IT Asset Manager for confirmation or substitution.

- Stock is validated at the joiner's primary location; a shortfall opens an Asset Procurement ticket against the Catalog Queue.

- Asset preparation reserves the physical asset (state → Reserved), images and installs software per bundle, and enables encryption per tenant default.

- Account provisioning grants identity, SaaS tokens, mailbox, and collaboration tools per the access matrix.

- On Day-1 handover (or shipped per the remote sub-flow), the Asset Acknowledgement Form is issued; on acknowledgement the asset moves to In Use.

- On completion the system emits m17.it_provisioning_complete to M02 with asset_ids and account references; M02 marks the onboarding task done.

**Business rules:**

- Default bundles (Phase 1): Standard Knowledge Worker, Engineering, Design, Sales / Field, Executive, Server-Ops, Network-Ops — contents finalised at implementation.

- Remote / out-of-location joiners use a shipped sub-flow: courier selected from the vendor master, shipment tracked, state Reserved → In Transit → In Use on delivery confirmation.

- Server / Network class assets use direct IT Asset Manager assignment in Phase 1 (Infrastructure Provisioning workflow is Phase 1.5).

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If the assigned asset is unavailable at task completion, IT Admin selects an alternative; the task does not block on stock.

- If the acknowledgement is not completed within 3 working days, IT Admin is notified and assignment_status remains Pending Acknowledgement.

**FR-M17-008: IT service desk with ticket lifecycle and Resolution Notes gate**

**Description: **The IT service desk manages Incident, Service Request, Access Request, Query/How-To, IT Grievance, and system-generated ticket types through an eleven-state lifecycle. The In Progress → Resolved transition is blocked until Resolution Notes are populated.

**User story: ***As a Service Desk Agent, I want a governed ticket lifecycle with a mandatory Resolution Notes gate so that every resolution captures root cause and is auditable.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Tickets are created via the self-service portal (Phase 1; email-to-ticket is Phase 2) and assigned a user-facing REQ-{YYYY}-{5d} ID mapped 1:1 to an internal SD-{YYYY}-{8d} ID.

- Ticket states: New, Open, In Progress, Pending, Pending Approval, Resolved, Closed, Cancelled, Out-of-Scope, Reopened — with allowed transitions enforced by the engine.

- The In Progress → Resolved transition is system-blocked until Resolution Notes (root cause, steps taken, parts replaced, follow-up actions) are populated — no agent workaround.

- Resolved tickets auto-close after a 3-business-day reopen window unless the requester confirms or reopens earlier.

- Pending state pauses SLA clocks; the Pending Reason sub-field captures Awaiting User / Awaiting Vendor / Awaiting Third Party, and Awaiting Vendor sets a vendor_caused flag for analytics.

- Duplicate detection at creation by creator + category + asset reference + 30-minute window suggests linking to the existing ticket.

- Every IT-asset-related ticket must cite an asset_id (validated for Hardware / Software / Network categories; optional for Account / Access and Service Request — Account).

**Business rules:**

- Ticket types: Incident, Service Request, Access Request, Change Request (Phase 1.5), Query / How-To, IT Grievance, Asset Audit Exception (system), Asset Recovery / Lost Asset (system).

- Tickets are not anonymous; creator identity is visible to all participants. IT Grievance tickets are visible only to the Service Desk Admin.

- CSAT (1–5 scale, low-score flag at < 3) fires on Closed, with a 72-hour submission window.

- Out-of-scope non-IT requests (HR / POSH / payroll) are marked Out-of-Scope by the Service Desk Admin with a redirect message.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If the ticket creator is separated, the ticket remains active, replies route to the manager, and CSAT is suppressed (EX-ITSM-04).

- If a cited KB article returns 404 in a resolution, the broken link is flagged and the KB owner notified; the resolution still stands (EX-ITSM-17).

- Tickets assigned to an agent who is on leave or who leaves mid-cycle are auto-reassigned within the same queue, retaining notes and context (EX-ITSM-06, EX-ITSM-23).

**FR-M17-009: Service desk SLA model, priority categories, and escalation matrix**

**Description: **Each ticket carries a response SLA and a resolution SLA measured in business hours (24×7 for P1). A category-and-queue register routes tickets by type, and an escalation matrix fires pre-breach alerts at 80% elapsed and tiered breach notifications.

**User story: ***As a Service Desk Lead, I want SLA timers, routing, and escalation built in so that priority work is met and breaches surface before they happen.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- SLA defaults: P1 15 min / 4 h (24×7); P2 1 h / 8 business hours; P3 4 business hours / 3 business days; P4 1 business day / 5 business days — all tenant-configurable by the Service Desk Admin.

- A pre-breach alert fires at 80% of SLA elapsed (configurable per category) to the Service Desk Lead plus the assignee, for both response and resolution timers.

- On breach, notifications fire to assignee + queue lead; at 2× SLA the Service Desk Admin is added; P1 unresolved beyond 2 hours triggers an immediate phone escalation to IT Director / CTO.

- Unassigned tickets older than 60 minutes notify the queue lead.

- Tickets are routed to default queues by category (Hardware, Software, Network, Identity, Catalog, Access, Asset, Security, Change, Generalist); mis-routed tickets allow up to 2 re-route hops.

- Default agent assignment within a queue is round-robin across agents on Active duty (skill-based routing is Phase 2).

**Business rules:**

- P1 categories (site-wide network, security) use a firm 24×7 SLA; the SLA clock does not pause outside business hours for P1.

- Security-category tickets bypass to the InfoSec Lead (CISO) with immediate phone + flag.

- Vendor-paused tickets (Pending = Awaiting Vendor) fire a Vendor Support escalation to the IT Asset Manager within 2 business hours.

- SLA timer accuracy is ± 30 seconds (± 5 seconds for P1).

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If an approver in an access chain is inactive, approval re-routes to the approver's manager; if no chain is available, the Service Desk Admin assumes approval authority with an audit trail (EX-ITSM-25).

- An agent's approved leave triggers auto-reassignment within 5 minutes of leave start (EX-ITSM-06).

**FR-M17-010: Service catalog with access-request approval matrix**

**Description: **A configurable service catalog offers defined request items (new asset, software access, account/access) each with a default audience, approval chain, fulfilment SLA, and form. Access-grant items route through a graduated approval matrix.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want a self-service catalog so that I can request hardware, software, and access through a guided form that routes to the right approvers.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Each catalog item carries: item ID, name, description, category, default audience, approval chain, fulfilment SLA, fulfilment queue, default priority, and item-specific form fields.

- The Phase 1 catalog includes sixteen items (e.g. New Laptop, Laptop refresh, New Mobile/SIM, Additional monitor, Peripheral, Docking station, External storage, Software install standard/non-standard, SaaS standard/restricted, VPN, AWS Read/Admin, Production DB Read, Source repo Admin).

- Access-grant items route through the approval matrix (e.g. VPN: L1 Manager → IT Admin; AWS Admin: L1 → Cloud Lead → CTO → IT Admin; Production DB Read: L1 → Data Lead → DPO → CISO → IT Admin).

- Fulfilment is a seven-step flow: select item → pre-fill from M01 → complete justification + form + cost centre → route to L1 → approval chain advances → fulfilment queue picks up → Resolved with fulfilment evidence.

- Catalog items in a user's default audience with no approval chain may auto-fulfil (e.g. standard software install).

**Business rules:**

- Catalog items, audiences, approval chains, and SLAs are tenant-configurable by the Service Desk Admin — no code change for routine tuning.

- The New Asset Request Form captures business justification (mandatory), urgency, required-by date, cost centre / budget code, and delivery location; if required-by < SLA the system flags 'Expedite required'.

- Cost centre drives chargeback reporting (Phase 2).

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a catalog item is out of stock, the Service Request moves to Pending (vendor_caused) and an Asset Procurement ticket opens in parallel (EX-ITSM-09).

- Access requests from a separating employee block at L1 with a 'requester is on notice' warning; the manager decides and the outcome is audit-logged (EX-ITSM-10).

**FR-M17-011: Knowledge base and self-service**

**Description: **A versioned knowledge base provides categorised, audience-scoped articles that power self-service resolution (password/MFA reset, catalog auto-fulfilment, audit self-attestation) and ground Query/How-To ticket resolution.

**User story: ***As an Employee, I want a searchable knowledge base and guided self-service so that I can resolve common IT issues without raising a ticket.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Each KB article carries: article ID (KB-{6d}), title, category and sub-category, audience, summary, body, tags, prerequisites, related articles, last-reviewed date and reviewer, and owner role.

- Articles are versioned; published versions are immutable and edits create a new version.

- Self-service surfaces at go-live: password reset (KB-guided with M01 identity verification), MFA reset, catalog auto-fulfilment, and annual audit self-attestation by Department Heads.

- Query / How-To tickets are resolved by linking to a KB article.

- KB search returns results across the article corpus at p95 < 600 ms.

**Business rules:**

- Forty articles across Account & Access, Hardware, Software, Network & Connectivity, Security, and Service Desk usage are authored as a pre-go-live deliverable.

- Default publishing model is author-publishes with Service Desk Admin audit (final position per DM-ITSM07).

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a KB article cited in a ticket resolution is later archived or broken, the resolution stands and the KB owner is notified to update the article.

**FR-M17-012: Configuration Management Database (CMDB)**

**Description: **The CMDB is the M17-internal Configuration Item store, populated from the asset master plus non-asset CIs (business services, locations, vendors, software, SaaS). CI relationships drive incident impact analysis, change impact assessment, and bundle-return analysis.

**User story: ***As a Service Desk Lead, I want a CMDB linking assets, services, and dependencies so that I can assess the blast radius of an incident or change.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Six CI types: Asset CI (from the asset master), Software CI, SaaS CI, Business Service CI, Location CI, Vendor CI.

- CI relationships: ASSIGNED_TO, LOCATED_AT, RUNS_ON, DEPENDS_ON, AFFECTED_BY, COMPONENT_OF.

- Incident impact analysis lists CIs AFFECTED_BY the affected service; change impact assessment lists affected CIs at Change Request submission.

- Bundle return analysis checks COMPONENT_OF children at FnF return and flags any not on the expected-returns list (e.g. a separately-issued docking station overlooked).

- Asset CIs reuse the AST-ID; non-asset CIs use CI-{8d}.

**Business rules:**

- Every IT-asset-related ticket must cite an asset_id — this linkage is the foundation of the CMDB.

- RUNS_ON (software-on-asset) relationships are reconciled at the annual audit.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a parent asset has an incident, the CMDB surfaces its components (docking station, monitor, headset) for impact context.

**FR-M17-013: P1 incident post-mortem**

**Description: **When a P1 incident ticket reaches Closed, the system automatically opens a post-mortem record pre-filled with the ticket timeline, root cause, affected scope, and SLA breach status, routed through a draft-review-sign-off flow ending in CTO sign-off.

**User story: ***As a CTO, I want an automatic post-mortem on every P1 incident so that root cause and recurrence-prevention are documented and tracked.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- On a ticket that was P1 at any point reaching Closed, the system opens a post-mortem (PM-{YYYY}-{4d}) pre-filled with timeline, root cause (from Resolution Notes), affected scope (from CMDB AFFECTED_BY), and SLA breach status.

- The Service Desk Lead drafts the post-mortem within 5 business days; assigned agents contribute timeline and root cause; the CISO contributes if security-adjacent.

- The IT Director reviews within 10 business days and the CTO signs off.

- Remediation actions are logged as separate tickets with target dates; recurrence-prevention items are added to backlog.

- The post-mortem PDF is retained 7 years.

**Business rules:**

- A post-mortem is mandatory for any ticket that held P1 priority at any point in its lifecycle, even if later downgraded.

- Resolution Notes must be captured (per the mandatory gate) before the post-mortem can be generated.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- If a post-mortem is overdue beyond 10 business days, the CTO is auto-notified and it is tracked as a non-compliance finding to closure within 30 days (EX-ITSM-20).

**FR-M17-014: Change management — CAB review — WITHDRAWN (out of scope; Change Request ticket type remains inactive, not built in Phase 1)**

**Description: Withdrawn. Change Advisory Board (CAB) and IT change management are not in scope. The Change Request ticket type and Infrastructure Provisioning workflow remain inactive.**

**User story: ***As a Change Manager, I want a CAB workflow so that non-standard changes to production CIs are reviewed, scheduled, and reversible with a post-implementation review.*

**Acceptance criteria:**

- Change Requests (CHG-{YYYY}-{6d}) are sub-typed Standard (pre-approved routine — patch, OS upgrade) or Non-Standard (configuration change requiring CAB review).

- Phase 1 captures change tickets for record only; Phase 1.5 runs the full CAB workflow on P01.

- Non-standard submission captures change description, business justification, technical plan, rollback plan, affected CIs, risk assessment, and planned change window.

- CAB membership (proposed CTO, CISO, IT Director, Infra Lead, Network Lead, IT Asset Manager; quorum 4 of 6) reviews asynchronously plus at the next CAB meeting; decisions are Approve / Approve with stipulations / Defer / Reject.

- On approval the change is scheduled, affected service owners are notified, and the outcome is captured (Successful / Partially successful / Rolled back).

**Business rules:**

- In Phase 1 the IT Asset Manager double-hats as Change Manager; a distinct Change Manager role is decided at Phase 1.5 design.

- A post-implementation review is mandatory for Rolled back or Partially successful changes.

**Edge cases ****&**** error handling:**

- Emergency changes bypass the standard CAB queue and require synchronous CTO + CISO approval, with a mandatory PIR within 5 business days (EX-ITSM-21).

- A change implementation failure with production impact auto-opens a linked P1 incident and marks the change Rolled back with PIR mandatory (EX-ITSM-22).

